{:db/id "step-8469b78a-c8a0-5898-ab81-fd8011c0d4cd" :step/id #uuid "8469b78a-c8a0-5898-ab81-fd8011c0d4cd" :step/content 17592186233094 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5a5f265a-f526-59c9-bd11-0cdcb7cdd9b5" :step/id #uuid "5a5f265a-f526-59c9-bd11-0cdcb7cdd9b5" :step/content 17592186512891 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-dcc9db76-a9ce-541f-a6be-f54b162a7c67" :step/id #uuid "dcc9db76-a9ce-541f-a6be-f54b162a7c67" :step/content 17592186512902 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168089 :tutorial/content "We can define the **reciprocal hyperbolic functions** similarly to how we define reciprocal trigonometric functions. \n\nThe **hyperbolic cosecant function** (pronounced \"cosetch\") is defined as \n![](images/c1/c112c1732880af3a22ea3831581062f4b7d8a96ad3408e9a98af0387691e9af7.svg)\nThe graph of ![](images/1d/1de5a1885eb15a44f9de1b9d170b8906f684c1784f5f67de840cca1628f2ec91.svg) is shown below.\n\n![](images/cc/cc0b2c19d1606c8a0a2c11411a0b0a4fbc09f264d9d28d0fcb2fb81f6c576a8a.png)\n\nNote the following properties of ![](images/ea/ea88889bed67f76a0de3b6f4d3acd48051d741d4c51b8013b69fd6642e30eac1.svg)\n\n- Its domain is ![](images/56/56c0f97156e3980b9cf25c7204e993714a0879ba463132c958267e5927e71f4c.svg)\n\n- Its range is ![](images/e8/e8eea575c16295c549b3019751f3562aa98b9d6f3e15375c24837521528878ca.svg)\n\n- It has a vertical asymptote ![](images/29/29da0a4605613d6a7fd9e7bb2f4fb45cbabe8a47109971682844cb222486a20c.svg)\n\n- It has a horizontal asymptote ![](images/6c/6c63827e94b6c11a9803605bd54156d90d3fcc6ff4639dd179a5fb024bcffaa1.svg)\n\n- It is an odd function." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168105 :tutorial/content "The **hyperbolic secant function** (pronounced \"sesh\") is defined as \n![](images/e7/e7c30afe931272f96d3af0ae8b020cd011c0d3a16bc299bf6afb58a3853d5923.svg)\nThe graph of ![](images/b6/b63b49cef7052e7402e9f894d46b94ffb2f406b51d57402272dba5aade93a508.svg) is shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b228f9fba9d1b981d64730de4b3836de914e2565332780a3fd803c165fb7d2ba.png)\n\nNote the following properties of ![](images/e8/e82bcae39f329ede047f75bb74921d7a2a9d55f74b529a98fee4581b87d35849.svg)\n\n- Its domain is ![](images/aa/aa2627839a0a228b0c54166eb26bf5943e21bbb690d71d1059b5bad6f36d72bd.svg)\n\n- Its range is ![](images/70/70c4b1eec58c8b8a94bc35b936d5d3ce5ea71f302517d16eb81e3fa7adf7afec.svg)\n\n- It has a horizontal asymptote ![](images/6c/6c63827e94b6c11a9803605bd54156d90d3fcc6ff4639dd179a5fb024bcffaa1.svg)\n\n- It is an even function." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168106 :tutorial/content "The **hyperbolic cotangent function** (pronounced \"coth\") is defined as \n![](images/a7/a76c7780cd40b24bf3cb5dcd55eecd4c14e4a79825cba1501d4b8afd425405ba.svg)\nThe graph of ![](images/15/158841441ef667264704ceb47ea5e26bac1385fef070fd7e42ae1527bada9631.svg) is shown below.\n\n![](images/d3/d3d4f5cbe846f7725399fa1b1769c4f476be439575043537a2caa80c318a5ec9.png)\n\nNote the following properties of ![](images/d9/d90a56456cf17cffd5fe59547103b45ba1ba186c40060f43e3674e66fcd69434.svg)\n\n- Its domain is ![](images/56/56c0f97156e3980b9cf25c7204e993714a0879ba463132c958267e5927e71f4c.svg)\n\n- Its range is ![](images/f1/f111c1f23a40b18e1e4baf575183e31bcc8d7987e90a8a16e024c252b61699d3.svg)\n\n- It has a vertical asymptote ![](images/29/29da0a4605613d6a7fd9e7bb2f4fb45cbabe8a47109971682844cb222486a20c.svg)\n\n- It has horizontal asymptotes ![](images/84/849544fed9b7437b1c0d262b2cf17d4539f767beab6ba7c8a7de82cff4c7ca68.svg)\n\n- It is an odd function.\n\nUsing the alternative definitions of ![](images/0d/0d5fcc991aa44ea723f4c42355e8a876b33441902dfb72ae82d4917ffff1b16b.svg) we can write down two more definitions for ![](images/94/944a08f36feb5caee9da43f160c130a4b48b46f5f28fe5be3147a272b600eb74.svg)\n![](images/d2/d26afd4eb7797e35fab8b46d018a646cf1faf02c7ffae9311587754ed210ef31.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167031 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064338 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-864d30c1-9b9e-5747-9ebf-06a4c172035c" "question-01c94ce2-04ad-5708-8ba5-8bfca9809325"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-864d30c1-9b9e-5747-9ebf-06a4c172035c" :question/id #uuid "864d30c1-9b9e-5747-9ebf-06a4c172035c" :question/math-academy-id "q-47870" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=3{x}^{2}$, what is ${f}^{′}(2)$ according to the definition?" :question/worked-solution "The definition of ${f}^{′}(2)$ is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}(\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}).\n$$\n\nLet $a=2.$ Then, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{x-2}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4fdf64b2-7dfc-5534-bdd0-a30466c74ca2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4fdf64b2-7dfc-5534-bdd0-a30466c74ca2" :answer-field/id #uuid "4fdf64b2-7dfc-5534-bdd0-a30466c74ca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4b9b3af5-c3c8-599f-a61a-22160b2775b9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-12cc6b99-6aa4-5495-b69d-7c177d70318b" "answer-896cf965-91e5-5f59-949b-28482f31bce3" "answer-4b9b3af5-c3c8-599f-a61a-22160b2775b9" "answer-dddc1051-6963-51f0-a801-9b25da83d366" "answer-2ef965a1-569c-55ee-955d-a534fc912f4b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12cc6b99-6aa4-5495-b69d-7c177d70318b" :answer/id #uuid "12cc6b99-6aa4-5495-b69d-7c177d70318b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 2}(\\frac{3{x}^{2}+3(2)^{2}}{x-2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-896cf965-91e5-5f59-949b-28482f31bce3" :answer/id #uuid "896cf965-91e5-5f59-949b-28482f31bce3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 2}(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{x+2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b9b3af5-c3c8-599f-a61a-22160b2775b9" :answer/id #uuid "4b9b3af5-c3c8-599f-a61a-22160b2775b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 2}(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{x-2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dddc1051-6963-51f0-a801-9b25da83d366" :answer/id #uuid "dddc1051-6963-51f0-a801-9b25da83d366" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 2}(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{{2}^{2}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ef965a1-569c-55ee-955d-a534fc912f4b" :answer/id #uuid "2ef965a1-569c-55ee-955d-a534fc912f4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to 2}(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-3(2)^{2}}{{x}^{2}})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064337 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7fa38cf7-46e9-5719-8fa2-6633394b103a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7fa38cf7-46e9-5719-8fa2-6633394b103a" :question/id #uuid "7fa38cf7-46e9-5719-8fa2-6633394b103a" :question/math-academy-id "q-47887" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}$, the instantaneous rate of change ${f}^{′}(-1)$ can be computed using the limit of a difference quotient. The first few steps of this calculation are shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-1+h)-f(-1)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(-1+h)^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{h} \\\\  & =…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinish the calculation, and hence find ${f}^{′}(-1).$" :question/worked-solution "By proceeding with the remaining calculations we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-1+h)-f(-1)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{(-1+h)^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{1-2h+{h}^{2}-1}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{h(-2+h)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(-2+h) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-26e0a8d8-3d22-5a55-a37b-cb46b000140b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-26e0a8d8-3d22-5a55-a37b-cb46b000140b" :answer-field/id #uuid "26e0a8d8-3d22-5a55-a37b-cb46b000140b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e0c75f0b-aea6-5e09-82a2-6259cf035e05" :answer-field/choices ["answer-274df812-d124-5f50-84ac-b7aa8d336139" "answer-e0c75f0b-aea6-5e09-82a2-6259cf035e05" "answer-fb249616-4f0b-5854-ba69-96ad9991479c" "answer-cd9f2d30-713a-5a24-ab5c-b09f90073991" "answer-428ea4b9-5f3f-5c17-8bf2-2a02357d0d8e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-274df812-d124-5f50-84ac-b7aa8d336139" :answer/id #uuid "274df812-d124-5f50-84ac-b7aa8d336139" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-1)=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0c75f0b-aea6-5e09-82a2-6259cf035e05" :answer/id #uuid "e0c75f0b-aea6-5e09-82a2-6259cf035e05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-1)=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb249616-4f0b-5854-ba69-96ad9991479c" :answer/id #uuid "fb249616-4f0b-5854-ba69-96ad9991479c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-1)=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd9f2d30-713a-5a24-ab5c-b09f90073991" :answer/id #uuid "cd9f2d30-713a-5a24-ab5c-b09f90073991" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-1)=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-428ea4b9-5f3f-5c17-8bf2-2a02357d0d8e" :answer/id #uuid "428ea4b9-5f3f-5c17-8bf2-2a02357d0d8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-1)=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-01c94ce2-04ad-5708-8ba5-8bfca9809325" :question/id #uuid "01c94ce2-04ad-5708-8ba5-8bfca9809325" :question/math-academy-id "q-101950" :question/problem "Find the instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)=5{x}^{2}$ at the point $x=1$ by computing the limit of a difference quotient." :question/worked-solution "*Method 1:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(1)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=5{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{5(1+h)^{2}-5(1)^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{5+10h+5{h}^{2}-5}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{10h+5{h}^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(10+5h) \\\\  & =10+(0) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n*Method 2:*\n\nBy the definition, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(1)={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{f(x)-f(1)}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=5{x}^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{5{x}^{2}-5(1)^{2}}{x-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{5({x}^{2}-1)}{x-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{5(x-1)(x+1)}{x-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}5(x+1) \\\\  & =5(1+1) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-140d3701-ba23-5c26-9cf9-6d695b5d1f47"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-140d3701-ba23-5c26-9cf9-6d695b5d1f47" :answer-field/id #uuid "140d3701-ba23-5c26-9cf9-6d695b5d1f47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5ed5baec-393d-5e5f-af72-0e15849dbfe0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-478dcde0-ffcc-5e5b-9556-15d26a321acd" "answer-e70f5fff-9528-5097-9f8b-f32c917cacb4" "answer-5ed5baec-393d-5e5f-af72-0e15849dbfe0" "answer-16929637-3274-5e13-ac66-9758de4de287" "answer-0fbcae67-db75-584e-abdf-39bdb2826748"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-478dcde0-ffcc-5e5b-9556-15d26a321acd" :answer/id #uuid "478dcde0-ffcc-5e5b-9556-15d26a321acd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(1)=-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e70f5fff-9528-5097-9f8b-f32c917cacb4" :answer/id #uuid "e70f5fff-9528-5097-9f8b-f32c917cacb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(1)=-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ed5baec-393d-5e5f-af72-0e15849dbfe0" :answer/id #uuid "5ed5baec-393d-5e5f-af72-0e15849dbfe0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(1)=10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16929637-3274-5e13-ac66-9758de4de287" :answer/id #uuid "16929637-3274-5e13-ac66-9758de4de287" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(1)=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fbcae67-db75-584e-abdf-39bdb2826748" :answer/id #uuid "0fbcae67-db75-584e-abdf-39bdb2826748" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{′}(1)=5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-a116c91c-02cc-5bc2-b27d-28406f69f493" :activity/id #uuid "a116c91c-02cc-5bc2-b27d-28406f69f493" :activity/title "The Instantaneous Rate of Change of a Function at a Point" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049362 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-3af6a943-98b1-5bd0-aab5-0234441f6c41" "step-2b61f38b-94db-5d5a-b982-f179ca27382b" "step-a2d1e0d7-fe00-50a9-bcc0-d71842a58b46"] :activity/first-step "step-3af6a943-98b1-5bd0-aab5-0234441f6c41" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-3af6a943-98b1-5bd0-aab5-0234441f6c41" :step/id #uuid "3af6a943-98b1-5bd0-aab5-0234441f6c41" :step/content "question-864d30c1-9b9e-5747-9ebf-06a4c172035c" :step/next "step-2b61f38b-94db-5d5a-b982-f179ca27382b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2b61f38b-94db-5d5a-b982-f179ca27382b" :step/id #uuid "2b61f38b-94db-5d5a-b982-f179ca27382b" :step/content "question-7fa38cf7-46e9-5719-8fa2-6633394b103a" :step/next "step-a2d1e0d7-fe00-50a9-bcc0-d71842a58b46" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a2d1e0d7-fe00-50a9-bcc0-d71842a58b46" :step/id #uuid "a2d1e0d7-fe00-50a9-bcc0-d71842a58b46" :step/content "question-01c94ce2-04ad-5708-8ba5-8bfca9809325" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198434 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f0b66b9d-4d88-5de4-a9f2-e4468c8b8ed2" "question-983968fc-15f6-5756-ab92-7399b778fa93"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f0b66b9d-4d88-5de4-a9f2-e4468c8b8ed2" :question/id #uuid "f0b66b9d-4d88-5de4-a9f2-e4468c8b8ed2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163105" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x+y+4},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2+ax+\\frac{1}{4}y-\\frac{1}{64}{x}^{2}+bxy+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y+4}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y+4}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =\\frac{1}{4}, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{(x+y+4)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{32} \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{(x+y+4)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{32} \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{(x+y+4)^{3}}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-\\frac{1}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈2+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot x+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{32}\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-\\frac{1}{32})\\cdot xy+(-\\frac{1}{32})\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{4}x+\\frac{1}{4}y-\\frac{1}{64}{x}^{2}+(-\\frac{1}{32})xy+(-\\frac{1}{64}){y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=\\frac{1}{4}+(-\\frac{1}{32})+(-\\frac{1}{64})=\\frac{13}{64}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-57708cab-0a02-5b87-93ec-6746644b7829"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-57708cab-0a02-5b87-93ec-6746644b7829" :answer-field/id #uuid "57708cab-0a02-5b87-93ec-6746644b7829" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b5cff024-e8a3-5830-b01f-71c191133afb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b5cff024-e8a3-5830-b01f-71c191133afb" "answer-213c31e4-604c-5637-a050-4eaeb6c0f751" "answer-b6db8036-e893-58df-8210-1dab0e50bf02" "answer-878b145b-533e-56a3-a50a-7a45a1f3deec" "answer-cb9dc1c3-ffa1-5373-bb29-a4874096f214"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5cff024-e8a3-5830-b01f-71c191133afb" :answer/id #uuid "b5cff024-e8a3-5830-b01f-71c191133afb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{13}{64}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-213c31e4-604c-5637-a050-4eaeb6c0f751" :answer/id #uuid "213c31e4-604c-5637-a050-4eaeb6c0f751" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15}{32}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6db8036-e893-58df-8210-1dab0e50bf02" :answer/id #uuid "b6db8036-e893-58df-8210-1dab0e50bf02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{25}{64}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-878b145b-533e-56a3-a50a-7a45a1f3deec" :answer/id #uuid "878b145b-533e-56a3-a50a-7a45a1f3deec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb9dc1c3-ffa1-5373-bb29-a4874096f214" :answer/id #uuid "cb9dc1c3-ffa1-5373-bb29-a4874096f214" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{32}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-983968fc-15f6-5756-ab92-7399b778fa93" :question/id #uuid "983968fc-15f6-5756-ab92-7399b778fa93" :question/math-academy-id "q-163108" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{e}^{y}),$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=ax+by-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+cxy.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{y}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{{e}^{y}}{x+{e}^{y}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{{e}^{y}}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{x{e}^{y}}{(x+{e}^{y})^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈0+1\\cdot x+1\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-1)\\cdot xy+0\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1x+1y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+(-1)xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+(-1)=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-be0946b1-502a-5aba-b006-539568a4bc82"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-be0946b1-502a-5aba-b006-539568a4bc82" :answer-field/id #uuid "be0946b1-502a-5aba-b006-539568a4bc82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-53608405-55af-52ab-8bb1-8ba8739d1150" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8572f2aa-9106-5587-bca8-e37536bbe74a" "answer-0d7d3174-3459-579a-b643-b960a7dd075c" "answer-53608405-55af-52ab-8bb1-8ba8739d1150" "answer-cd348c35-901b-57a1-93a1-95ed5535c117" "answer-5d9e8b65-e8ee-589b-9251-9db47e2725f7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8572f2aa-9106-5587-bca8-e37536bbe74a" :answer/id #uuid "8572f2aa-9106-5587-bca8-e37536bbe74a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d7d3174-3459-579a-b643-b960a7dd075c" :answer/id #uuid "0d7d3174-3459-579a-b643-b960a7dd075c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53608405-55af-52ab-8bb1-8ba8739d1150" :answer/id #uuid "53608405-55af-52ab-8bb1-8ba8739d1150" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd348c35-901b-57a1-93a1-95ed5535c117" :answer/id #uuid "cd348c35-901b-57a1-93a1-95ed5535c117" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d9e8b65-e8ee-589b-9251-9db47e2725f7" :answer/id #uuid "5d9e8b65-e8ee-589b-9251-9db47e2725f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-df60b178-1cb5-516a-821d-f1274833f6f6" :activity/id #uuid "df60b178-1cb5-516a-821d-f1274833f6f6" :activity/title "Second-Degree Taylor Polynomials of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048786 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-1351f0da-3665-50fe-aa04-9f12a6f4264c" "step-ab8b3d07-995c-53ab-94ae-6bf1bafbde24" "step-495367cd-f07e-5132-8f32-4e563019fabe" "step-92a16d7f-ef9b-52e3-b0ea-6bfb381ddffb" "step-c41363ef-ff87-5fdf-9c49-497841beb370"] :activity/first-step "step-1351f0da-3665-50fe-aa04-9f12a6f4264c" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-1351f0da-3665-50fe-aa04-9f12a6f4264c" :step/id #uuid "1351f0da-3665-50fe-aa04-9f12a6f4264c" :step/content 17592186281595 :step/next "step-ab8b3d07-995c-53ab-94ae-6bf1bafbde24" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ab8b3d07-995c-53ab-94ae-6bf1bafbde24" :step/id #uuid "ab8b3d07-995c-53ab-94ae-6bf1bafbde24" :step/content "question-f0b66b9d-4d88-5de4-a9f2-e4468c8b8ed2" :step/next "step-495367cd-f07e-5132-8f32-4e563019fabe" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-495367cd-f07e-5132-8f32-4e563019fabe" :step/id #uuid "495367cd-f07e-5132-8f32-4e563019fabe" :step/content "question-983968fc-15f6-5756-ab92-7399b778fa93" :step/next "step-92a16d7f-ef9b-52e3-b0ea-6bfb381ddffb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-92a16d7f-ef9b-52e3-b0ea-6bfb381ddffb" :step/id #uuid "92a16d7f-ef9b-52e3-b0ea-6bfb381ddffb" :step/content 17592186516867 :step/next "step-c41363ef-ff87-5fdf-9c49-497841beb370" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c41363ef-ff87-5fdf-9c49-497841beb370" :step/id #uuid "c41363ef-ff87-5fdf-9c49-497841beb370" :step/content 17592186284523 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162129 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-db6164d4-0ab9-5b8d-a944-50886d9aea3b" "question-33de5000-925c-5b02-ae4e-01b5310d9ba9" "question-a3d747d1-36eb-5ac7-aecf-ff0858227dfd" "question-0729c3e7-6c49-5561-ba72-ac80cc555328"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161695 :question/problem "![](images/e5/e5f658b08e954097ef9ed39ebb935e800251a2ef729a02bc18240a0ca89167cc.svg) \n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. Find the conditional expected value $\\textrm{E}\\big[Y \\,|\\, X=1\\big]$ given that the conditional probability density function $f_{Y|X}(y \\,|\\, 1)$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E\\big[ Y \\,|\\, X = x \\big] = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} y \\, f_{Y|X}(y \\,|\\, x) \\; \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{3(1+{y}^{2})}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162127 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b25c780b-4145-5bfa-8e04-0856e9d67d3a" "question-28a2968a-07fa-5df6-a93c-3d505caa4a83" "question-4aa42e03-e8d1-5333-8d07-d9074e34a91f" "question-13aa670b-3e27-50cc-b24b-f562a4443719" "question-b86ab1d5-8833-598a-9757-b29ccf7f9acc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161693 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y-x, & -1\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}+y, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $Y$ are shown above. Find the conditional expected value $\\textrm{E}\\bigg[X \\,\\bigg|\\, Y=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\bigg].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E\\big[ X \\,|\\, Y = y \\big] = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x \\, f_{X|Y}(x \\,|\\, y) \\; \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\textrm E\\big[ X \\,|\\, Y = y \\big]$ is a function of $y.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{y-x}{\\frac{1}{2}+y} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(y-x)}{1+2y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $-1 \\leq x \\leq 0, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ Outside this domain, we have $f_{X|Y} (x \\,|\\, y) = 0.$\n\nNow, substituting $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the expression for $f_{X|Y} (x \\,|\\, y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{2(\\frac{1}{2}-x)}{1+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{2}] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}x(\\frac{1}{2}-x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(\\frac{x}{2}-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162128 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4a9126d5-9f56-54dd-b358-d393eac16b93" "question-200b9c27-2539-5e86-b17a-fa5314a06310" "question-7abac60c-426c-5228-9bff-bdb217864577" "question-5142c769-0ab4-5c62-90f2-d5f937ec0c66" "question-4c1f6156-411f-54e4-bb68-bbf84af25120"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161694 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{4}, & 0\\le y\\le x,\\,\\,0<x<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}, & 0<x<2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the expression for the conditional expected value $\\textrm{E}\\big[Y \\,|\\, X=x\\big]$ for $0 <     x <     2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E\\big[ Y \\,|\\, X = x \\big] = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} y \\, f_{Y|X}(y \\,|\\, x) \\; \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\textrm E\\big[ Y \\,|\\, X = x \\big]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{x+y}{4})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+y)}{3{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\leq y \\leq x,\\; 0 <              x <             2.$ Outside this domain, we have $f_{Y|X} (y \\,|\\, x) = 0.$ \n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}y\\cdot \\,\\frac{2(x+y)}{3{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(xy+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3{x}^{2}}{[\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3{x}^{2}}[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{5{x}^{3}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{5{x}^{2}\\cdot x}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{5\\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot x}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-db6164d4-0ab9-5b8d-a944-50886d9aea3b" :question/id #uuid "db6164d4-0ab9-5b8d-a944-50886d9aea3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-146180" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,1)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+{y}^{2}}{12}, & \\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\cdot \\frac{1+{y}^{2}}{12}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(y+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}(99-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6f03a1d0-67c6-5c08-a36d-a749c3ea0785"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6f03a1d0-67c6-5c08-a36d-a749c3ea0785" :answer-field/id #uuid "6f03a1d0-67c6-5c08-a36d-a749c3ea0785" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-136bf4b6-26e2-5491-9a45-4dc0a744ce96" :answer-field/choices ["answer-48b03958-5e31-5aeb-a187-15ffa3fb9dfd" "answer-b4a68f91-be81-5c57-9644-bf8d7a859773" "answer-136bf4b6-26e2-5491-9a45-4dc0a744ce96" "answer-a6d48e89-2b69-5bc2-931c-f7aff7dddcd0" "answer-c61dfa67-f506-5960-a9d7-adb05ec9f915"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48b03958-5e31-5aeb-a187-15ffa3fb9dfd" :answer/id #uuid "48b03958-5e31-5aeb-a187-15ffa3fb9dfd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4a68f91-be81-5c57-9644-bf8d7a859773" :answer/id #uuid "b4a68f91-be81-5c57-9644-bf8d7a859773" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-136bf4b6-26e2-5491-9a45-4dc0a744ce96" :answer/id #uuid "136bf4b6-26e2-5491-9a45-4dc0a744ce96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{33}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6d48e89-2b69-5bc2-931c-f7aff7dddcd0" :answer/id #uuid "a6d48e89-2b69-5bc2-931c-f7aff7dddcd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c61dfa67-f506-5960-a9d7-adb05ec9f915" :answer/id #uuid "c61dfa67-f506-5960-a9d7-adb05ec9f915" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-33de5000-925c-5b02-ae4e-01b5310d9ba9" :question/id #uuid "33de5000-925c-5b02-ae4e-01b5310d9ba9" :question/math-academy-id "q-146188" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,1)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2+2y}{3}, & \\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{2+2y}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a1557da6-b2bd-5567-b11c-16c8afd17b98"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a1557da6-b2bd-5567-b11c-16c8afd17b98" :answer-field/id #uuid "a1557da6-b2bd-5567-b11c-16c8afd17b98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3e481426-5239-5f6c-8193-9deaaf8eef05" :answer-field/choices ["answer-63999174-c937-5b70-8f31-1c0a52497413" "answer-4dd2746a-eb77-58a0-8586-c29564895209" "answer-3e481426-5239-5f6c-8193-9deaaf8eef05" "answer-db0f44e2-0364-5450-843f-ba45ebf9b0a6" "answer-1813320d-4887-55b9-9dad-afa95a010f45"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63999174-c937-5b70-8f31-1c0a52497413" :answer/id #uuid "63999174-c937-5b70-8f31-1c0a52497413" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4dd2746a-eb77-58a0-8586-c29564895209" :answer/id #uuid "4dd2746a-eb77-58a0-8586-c29564895209" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e481426-5239-5f6c-8193-9deaaf8eef05" :answer/id #uuid "3e481426-5239-5f6c-8193-9deaaf8eef05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db0f44e2-0364-5450-843f-ba45ebf9b0a6" :answer/id #uuid "db0f44e2-0364-5450-843f-ba45ebf9b0a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1813320d-4887-55b9-9dad-afa95a010f45" :answer/id #uuid "1813320d-4887-55b9-9dad-afa95a010f45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a3d747d1-36eb-5ac7-aecf-ff0858227dfd" :question/id #uuid "a3d747d1-36eb-5ac7-aecf-ff0858227dfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-146177" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,3)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}, & \\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=3]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,3)$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=3] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{32}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{32}-\\frac{0}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c1e39338-61c3-579d-a5f3-089f42737d5e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c1e39338-61c3-579d-a5f3-089f42737d5e" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1e39338-61c3-579d-a5f3-089f42737d5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-77133d69-998c-59fb-b57b-d052bbea19f1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-abc22133-fdf5-57dd-8c1c-1501423e681c" "answer-5619230d-a436-5ed6-8a1e-b2a127ca19a2" "answer-c676628d-a2e3-5f46-80cd-2839d9b7b1b2" "answer-77133d69-998c-59fb-b57b-d052bbea19f1" "answer-24f3a31a-ee9a-521d-b968-a205010ab955"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-abc22133-fdf5-57dd-8c1c-1501423e681c" :answer/id #uuid "abc22133-fdf5-57dd-8c1c-1501423e681c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5619230d-a436-5ed6-8a1e-b2a127ca19a2" :answer/id #uuid "5619230d-a436-5ed6-8a1e-b2a127ca19a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c676628d-a2e3-5f46-80cd-2839d9b7b1b2" :answer/id #uuid "c676628d-a2e3-5f46-80cd-2839d9b7b1b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77133d69-998c-59fb-b57b-d052bbea19f1" :answer/id #uuid "77133d69-998c-59fb-b57b-d052bbea19f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24f3a31a-ee9a-521d-b968-a205010ab955" :answer/id #uuid "24f3a31a-ee9a-521d-b968-a205010ab955" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0729c3e7-6c49-5561-ba72-ac80cc555328" :question/id #uuid "0729c3e7-6c49-5561-ba72-ac80cc555328" :question/math-academy-id "q-146238" :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,1.5)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(1+2{y}^{2})}{5}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. Find the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1.5]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1.5)$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1.5] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1.5)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{3(1+2{y}^{2})}{5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y+2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cc4a1905-341f-5668-92f7-b536136635df"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cc4a1905-341f-5668-92f7-b536136635df" :answer-field/id #uuid "cc4a1905-341f-5668-92f7-b536136635df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-acb66d99-3833-5c6b-8869-35f24a432fe6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-15d71dd1-c21c-5526-a084-8500b3aafe65" "answer-03237e50-d501-53e6-bba6-d412fef03733" "answer-55acfb92-edbe-511d-ae97-c980cd7635ed" "answer-acb66d99-3833-5c6b-8869-35f24a432fe6" "answer-675b9382-7ebc-5320-991c-3b7b3054e588"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15d71dd1-c21c-5526-a084-8500b3aafe65" :answer/id #uuid "15d71dd1-c21c-5526-a084-8500b3aafe65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03237e50-d501-53e6-bba6-d412fef03733" :answer/id #uuid "03237e50-d501-53e6-bba6-d412fef03733" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55acfb92-edbe-511d-ae97-c980cd7635ed" :answer/id #uuid "55acfb92-edbe-511d-ae97-c980cd7635ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-acb66d99-3833-5c6b-8869-35f24a432fe6" :answer/id #uuid "acb66d99-3833-5c6b-8869-35f24a432fe6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-675b9382-7ebc-5320-991c-3b7b3054e588" :answer/id #uuid "675b9382-7ebc-5320-991c-3b7b3054e588" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162417 :question/problem "$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. \nFind the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=2]$ given that the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=2] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\cdot \\frac{x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{12}-\\frac{1}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159704 :answer-field/correct 17592186159708 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-b25c780b-4145-5bfa-8e04-0856e9d67d3a" :question/id #uuid "b25c780b-4145-5bfa-8e04-0856e9d67d3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-146253" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3y}{12}, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+1}{4}, & 0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $Y$ are shown above. Find the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ is a function of $y.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2x+3y}{12})}{(\\frac{y+1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+3y}{3y+3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=0.$\n\nNow, substituting $y=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)=\\frac{2x+3\\cdot 1}{3\\cdot 1+3}=\\frac{2x+3}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{2x+3}{6}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(2{x}^{2}+3x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}((\\frac{16}{3}+6)-(\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{3}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{55}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ce1f1101-3df3-5c9e-9c08-818c39307178"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ce1f1101-3df3-5c9e-9c08-818c39307178" :answer-field/id #uuid "ce1f1101-3df3-5c9e-9c08-818c39307178" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4661b057-e34b-5ad1-86ad-f2170ff87ca2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ef193c69-7a43-5081-98b8-a77c6b8cc73b" "answer-1ddc283e-0948-540b-b84c-d2835daf8f6f" "answer-416e4a5c-22d6-5be2-bec7-c3eae49ba017" "answer-0d6fd54d-4c1c-55c6-8760-81621dbc6cde" "answer-4661b057-e34b-5ad1-86ad-f2170ff87ca2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef193c69-7a43-5081-98b8-a77c6b8cc73b" :answer/id #uuid "ef193c69-7a43-5081-98b8-a77c6b8cc73b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ddc283e-0948-540b-b84c-d2835daf8f6f" :answer/id #uuid "1ddc283e-0948-540b-b84c-d2835daf8f6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{14}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-416e4a5c-22d6-5be2-bec7-c3eae49ba017" :answer/id #uuid "416e4a5c-22d6-5be2-bec7-c3eae49ba017" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{25}{18}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d6fd54d-4c1c-55c6-8760-81621dbc6cde" :answer/id #uuid "0d6fd54d-4c1c-55c6-8760-81621dbc6cde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{31}{18}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4661b057-e34b-5ad1-86ad-f2170ff87ca2" :answer/id #uuid "4661b057-e34b-5ad1-86ad-f2170ff87ca2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{55}{36}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-28a2968a-07fa-5df6-a93c-3d505caa4a83" :question/id #uuid "28a2968a-07fa-5df6-a93c-3d505caa4a83" :question/math-academy-id "q-146345" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}12xy, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{5}, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12xy}{6{x}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $0<x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|1\\,)=2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot (2y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7b646b3b-c884-5b9e-bf55-39ae165ad7cb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7b646b3b-c884-5b9e-bf55-39ae165ad7cb" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b646b3b-c884-5b9e-bf55-39ae165ad7cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-816c831b-ddb4-5f28-8334-ebe8e11c9a11" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d716ab8f-83c1-5df4-9c34-94965f151237" "answer-432f54a1-0935-5f8b-8bdb-b406503cc001" "answer-816c831b-ddb4-5f28-8334-ebe8e11c9a11" "answer-45744fcc-0b9f-52ee-b902-f9e64f031117" "answer-fad6c54a-1f9e-59a7-8d04-ec373071feac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d716ab8f-83c1-5df4-9c34-94965f151237" :answer/id #uuid "d716ab8f-83c1-5df4-9c34-94965f151237" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-432f54a1-0935-5f8b-8bdb-b406503cc001" :answer/id #uuid "432f54a1-0935-5f8b-8bdb-b406503cc001" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-816c831b-ddb4-5f28-8334-ebe8e11c9a11" :answer/id #uuid "816c831b-ddb4-5f28-8334-ebe8e11c9a11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45744fcc-0b9f-52ee-b902-f9e64f031117" :answer/id #uuid "45744fcc-0b9f-52ee-b902-f9e64f031117" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fad6c54a-1f9e-59a7-8d04-ec373071feac" :answer/id #uuid "fad6c54a-1f9e-59a7-8d04-ec373071feac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4aa42e03-e8d1-5333-8d07-d9074e34a91f" :question/id #uuid "4aa42e03-e8d1-5333-8d07-d9074e34a91f" :question/math-academy-id "q-146254" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2-x-y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}-y, & 0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $Y$ are shown above. Find the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ is a function of $y.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2-x-y)}{(\\frac{3}{2}-y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-2x-2y}{3-2y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=0.$\n\nNow, substituting $y=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)=\\frac{4-2x-2\\cdot 1}{3-2\\cdot 1}=\\frac{4-2x-2}{3-2}=2-2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(2-2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x-2{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8527a0b3-bf88-546e-9eb5-59cbca7c2a30"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8527a0b3-bf88-546e-9eb5-59cbca7c2a30" :answer-field/id #uuid "8527a0b3-bf88-546e-9eb5-59cbca7c2a30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1a683496-0c43-5971-b86f-6d13331e35b1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-daf6bfcb-1f9b-5de5-bb85-1dda5505c8a0" "answer-1a683496-0c43-5971-b86f-6d13331e35b1" "answer-3307e64e-4740-5753-a998-4d2bb44bacd9" "answer-b0cc6f69-e221-5d61-9deb-81c1dda05489" "answer-b1c5dbb7-48c6-5964-a81c-58dd2ea3eb31"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-daf6bfcb-1f9b-5de5-bb85-1dda5505c8a0" :answer/id #uuid "daf6bfcb-1f9b-5de5-bb85-1dda5505c8a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a683496-0c43-5971-b86f-6d13331e35b1" :answer/id #uuid "1a683496-0c43-5971-b86f-6d13331e35b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3307e64e-4740-5753-a998-4d2bb44bacd9" :answer/id #uuid "3307e64e-4740-5753-a998-4d2bb44bacd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0cc6f69-e221-5d61-9deb-81c1dda05489" :answer/id #uuid "b0cc6f69-e221-5d61-9deb-81c1dda05489" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1c5dbb7-48c6-5964-a81c-58dd2ea3eb31" :answer/id #uuid "b1c5dbb7-48c6-5964-a81c-58dd2ea3eb31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-13aa670b-3e27-50cc-b24b-f562a4443719" :question/id #uuid "13aa670b-3e27-50cc-b24b-f562a4443719" :question/math-academy-id "q-146337" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(x+{y}^{2})}{8}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+1}{8}, & 0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3x+{y}^{2}}{8})}{(\\frac{3x+1}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(x+{y}^{2})}{3x+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$\n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)=\\frac{3(1+{y}^{2})}{3\\cdot 1+1}=\\frac{3(1+{y}^{2})}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot \\frac{3(1+{y}^{2})}{4}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y+{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}[(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ec37af3f-2077-53c3-aed3-459ba8fcb5e7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ec37af3f-2077-53c3-aed3-459ba8fcb5e7" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec37af3f-2077-53c3-aed3-459ba8fcb5e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dedd649a-149f-5255-a5fc-3c20f9338e9b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3b420ea0-e242-5852-9a3b-163544334e68" "answer-fbc01020-4089-56e6-82f5-6c8e9efb10f9" "answer-4d1c5347-c759-549b-a191-bc31ec802b90" "answer-e6bfe5a6-d3b1-5081-ab2c-efd0c7a6f4b1" "answer-dedd649a-149f-5255-a5fc-3c20f9338e9b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b420ea0-e242-5852-9a3b-163544334e68" :answer/id #uuid "3b420ea0-e242-5852-9a3b-163544334e68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbc01020-4089-56e6-82f5-6c8e9efb10f9" :answer/id #uuid "fbc01020-4089-56e6-82f5-6c8e9efb10f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d1c5347-c759-549b-a191-bc31ec802b90" :answer/id #uuid "4d1c5347-c759-549b-a191-bc31ec802b90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6bfe5a6-d3b1-5081-ab2c-efd0c7a6f4b1" :answer/id #uuid "e6bfe5a6-d3b1-5081-ab2c-efd0c7a6f4b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dedd649a-149f-5255-a5fc-3c20f9338e9b" :answer/id #uuid "dedd649a-149f-5255-a5fc-3c20f9338e9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b86ab1d5-8833-598a-9757-b29ccf7f9acc" :question/id #uuid "b86ab1d5-8833-598a-9757-b29ccf7f9acc" :question/math-academy-id "q-146360" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}8xy, & 0<x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}, & 0<x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8xy}{4{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $0<x\\le 1,\\,0<y\\le x.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$    \n\nNow, substituting $x=1$ into the expression for ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|1\\,)=2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot (2y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fd3b5bb7-8ff5-583c-95ec-4d9a61d8d3e9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fd3b5bb7-8ff5-583c-95ec-4d9a61d8d3e9" :answer-field/id #uuid "fd3b5bb7-8ff5-583c-95ec-4d9a61d8d3e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8220d898-eba8-5608-acb9-26ef0ebbb5ce" :answer-field/choices ["answer-626e204c-7f48-5f37-9d37-b43ff67dd7df" "answer-8220d898-eba8-5608-acb9-26ef0ebbb5ce" "answer-f88a890a-b584-5141-85df-189f2967c419" "answer-9246a3b3-e505-5724-80de-0b473089adf7" "answer-7fefcb29-5c38-5744-a9ee-086455686bd4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-626e204c-7f48-5f37-9d37-b43ff67dd7df" :answer/id #uuid "626e204c-7f48-5f37-9d37-b43ff67dd7df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8220d898-eba8-5608-acb9-26ef0ebbb5ce" :answer/id #uuid "8220d898-eba8-5608-acb9-26ef0ebbb5ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f88a890a-b584-5141-85df-189f2967c419" :answer/id #uuid "f88a890a-b584-5141-85df-189f2967c419" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9246a3b3-e505-5724-80de-0b473089adf7" :answer/id #uuid "9246a3b3-e505-5724-80de-0b473089adf7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fefcb29-5c38-5744-a9ee-086455686bd4" :answer/id #uuid "7fefcb29-5c38-5744-a9ee-086455686bd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4a9126d5-9f56-54dd-b358-d393eac16b93" :question/id #uuid "4a9126d5-9f56-54dd-b358-d393eac16b93" :question/math-academy-id "q-146433" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}, & 0\\le y\\le 4,\\,0<x<\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}24{x}^{2}, & 0<x<\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the expression for the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ for $0<x<\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{24{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le y\\le 4,\\,0<x<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\cdot \\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{16}{2}-0] \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9fa98042-2de8-53a0-9725-b83197c63351"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9fa98042-2de8-53a0-9725-b83197c63351" :answer-field/id #uuid "9fa98042-2de8-53a0-9725-b83197c63351" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9ec52a3e-3a84-504f-a2f1-f568cc685635" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2264b36a-087e-594e-9217-a261fcae3477" "answer-9ec52a3e-3a84-504f-a2f1-f568cc685635" "answer-90b18d84-cb8b-5c38-81d9-e6a9d91dbc74" "answer-3d8d2dd6-e5cd-5281-b1e3-60c2038d557b" "answer-6b7eaf44-8b6d-58de-8df7-7db229ea766f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2264b36a-087e-594e-9217-a261fcae3477" :answer/id #uuid "2264b36a-087e-594e-9217-a261fcae3477" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ec52a3e-3a84-504f-a2f1-f568cc685635" :answer/id #uuid "9ec52a3e-3a84-504f-a2f1-f568cc685635" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90b18d84-cb8b-5c38-81d9-e6a9d91dbc74" :answer/id #uuid "90b18d84-cb8b-5c38-81d9-e6a9d91dbc74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d8d2dd6-e5cd-5281-b1e3-60c2038d557b" :answer/id #uuid "3d8d2dd6-e5cd-5281-b1e3-60c2038d557b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b7eaf44-8b6d-58de-8df7-7db229ea766f" :answer/id #uuid "6b7eaf44-8b6d-58de-8df7-7db229ea766f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-200b9c27-2539-5e86-b17a-fa5314a06310" :question/id #uuid "200b9c27-2539-5e86-b17a-fa5314a06310" :question/math-academy-id "q-146517" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6(x+y)}{5}, & x\\le y\\le 2x,\\,0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the expression for the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ for $0<x<1.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{6(x+y)}{5})}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+y)}{5{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\le y\\le 2x,\\,0<x<1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{x}^{2x}y\\cdot \\,\\frac{2(x+y)}{5{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{x}^{2x}(xy+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5{x}^{2}}{[\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{x}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5{x}^{2}}[(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{2}+\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3})-(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{23{x}^{3}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{23x\\cdot \\cancel{({x}^{2})}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{23x}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8f740bd4-a687-5983-91a3-7513fe971934"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8f740bd4-a687-5983-91a3-7513fe971934" :answer-field/id #uuid "8f740bd4-a687-5983-91a3-7513fe971934" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fa62812a-5ad0-5c54-8abe-7a6dfef958dc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fa62812a-5ad0-5c54-8abe-7a6dfef958dc" "answer-da064ca0-ec2d-5668-b781-affcc343f33c" "answer-b9ece17e-ce07-5a63-9272-3986c9fc0b28" "answer-49e3ff31-b227-5b2a-8fe3-d34a74248ace" "answer-8262ba09-25dc-5226-a050-4bcaa05979b4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa62812a-5ad0-5c54-8abe-7a6dfef958dc" :answer/id #uuid "fa62812a-5ad0-5c54-8abe-7a6dfef958dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{23x}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da064ca0-ec2d-5668-b781-affcc343f33c" :answer/id #uuid "da064ca0-ec2d-5668-b781-affcc343f33c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2x+y}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9ece17e-ce07-5a63-9272-3986c9fc0b28" :answer/id #uuid "b9ece17e-ce07-5a63-9272-3986c9fc0b28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49e3ff31-b227-5b2a-8fe3-d34a74248ace" :answer/id #uuid "49e3ff31-b227-5b2a-8fe3-d34a74248ace" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8262ba09-25dc-5226-a050-4bcaa05979b4" :answer/id #uuid "8262ba09-25dc-5226-a050-4bcaa05979b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x+y}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7abac60c-426c-5228-9bff-bdb217864577" :question/id #uuid "7abac60c-426c-5228-9bff-bdb217864577" :question/math-academy-id "q-146397" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{4}, & 0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & 0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $X$ are shown above. Find the expression for the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ for $0<x<1.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x]$ is a function of $x.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{4})}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0<x<1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }y\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\cdot \\,\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{8}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}[4-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3a063c31-7ea1-511c-9093-dca00ca75be6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3a063c31-7ea1-511c-9093-dca00ca75be6" :answer-field/id #uuid "3a063c31-7ea1-511c-9093-dca00ca75be6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-51d6868e-9f63-566b-8657-3e46a79dcf7f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4b7b8be0-cd7d-5f2a-8d07-58b09b3e02bd" "answer-a358ba73-65d0-5ebe-ae65-bacd7eb25e86" "answer-cf07accb-49d6-5bf1-b6e5-cfad1ed38be2" "answer-51d6868e-9f63-566b-8657-3e46a79dcf7f" "answer-a6fae95c-8607-5baf-bcfe-438d0e382039"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b7b8be0-cd7d-5f2a-8d07-58b09b3e02bd" :answer/id #uuid "4b7b8be0-cd7d-5f2a-8d07-58b09b3e02bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a358ba73-65d0-5ebe-ae65-bacd7eb25e86" :answer/id #uuid "a358ba73-65d0-5ebe-ae65-bacd7eb25e86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf07accb-49d6-5bf1-b6e5-cfad1ed38be2" :answer/id #uuid "cf07accb-49d6-5bf1-b6e5-cfad1ed38be2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51d6868e-9f63-566b-8657-3e46a79dcf7f" :answer/id #uuid "51d6868e-9f63-566b-8657-3e46a79dcf7f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6fae95c-8607-5baf-bcfe-438d0e382039" :answer/id #uuid "a6fae95c-8607-5baf-bcfe-438d0e382039" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5142c769-0ab4-5c62-90f2-d5f937ec0c66" :question/id #uuid "5142c769-0ab4-5c62-90f2-d5f937ec0c66" :question/math-academy-id "q-146538" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(x+2y)}{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+4y}{3}, & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $Y$ are shown above. Find the expression for the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ for $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ is a function of $y.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $X$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2(x+2y)}{3})}{(\\frac{1+4y}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+2y)}{1+4y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot \\,\\frac{2(x+2y)}{1+4y}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+4y}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+2yx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+4y}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+y{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+4y}\\cdot [\\frac{1}{3}+y-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+4y}\\cdot \\frac{1+3y}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1+3y)}{3(1+4y)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e1a039c4-3510-5a92-9df7-feb0d8d5d857"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e1a039c4-3510-5a92-9df7-feb0d8d5d857" :answer-field/id #uuid "e1a039c4-3510-5a92-9df7-feb0d8d5d857" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-db470a65-0a51-550a-8ffd-cf65f10f85a6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5345dbd5-7e99-5317-8490-c789146a8788" "answer-e1e56427-f6a7-5af0-99d9-80933b07e001" "answer-1bf9c026-9fde-556b-a693-fbda3046a227" "answer-46d79442-5892-5af0-818d-d2ead962340e" "answer-db470a65-0a51-550a-8ffd-cf65f10f85a6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5345dbd5-7e99-5317-8490-c789146a8788" :answer/id #uuid "5345dbd5-7e99-5317-8490-c789146a8788" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(1+3y)}{(1+4y)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1e56427-f6a7-5af0-99d9-80933b07e001" :answer/id #uuid "e1e56427-f6a7-5af0-99d9-80933b07e001" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(1+3y)}{3(1+4y)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1bf9c026-9fde-556b-a693-fbda3046a227" :answer/id #uuid "1bf9c026-9fde-556b-a693-fbda3046a227" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(1+4y)}{(1+3y)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46d79442-5892-5af0-818d-d2ead962340e" :answer/id #uuid "46d79442-5892-5af0-818d-d2ead962340e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(y+3)}{3(y+4)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db470a65-0a51-550a-8ffd-cf65f10f85a6" :answer/id #uuid "db470a65-0a51-550a-8ffd-cf65f10f85a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2(1+3y)}{3(1+4y)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4c1f6156-411f-54e4-bb68-bbf84af25120" :question/id #uuid "4c1f6156-411f-54e4-bb68-bbf84af25120" :question/math-academy-id "q-146520" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6{x}^{2}+6y}{5}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6y+2}{5}, & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$  be continuous random variables. Their joint probability density function and the marginal probability density function of $Y$ are shown above. Find the expression for the conditional expected value $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ for $0<y<1.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y]$ is a function of $y.$ \n\nThe conditional density function of $X$ given $X=x$ can be computed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{6{x}^{2}+6y}{5})}{(\\frac{6y+2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}+6y}{6y+2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$ Outside this domain, we have ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, using the definition of the conditional expectation above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot \\,\\frac{6{x}^{2}+6y}{6y+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6y+2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}+xy)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6y+2}{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6y+2}[\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{y}{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6y+2}\\cdot \\frac{1+2y}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2(3y+1)}\\cdot \\frac{(1+2y)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1+2y)}{4(3y+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3d5798b6-1edf-5ed2-bbb7-aae35b6bf653"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3d5798b6-1edf-5ed2-bbb7-aae35b6bf653" :answer-field/id #uuid "3d5798b6-1edf-5ed2-bbb7-aae35b6bf653" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-445f339a-c1d1-5908-9ed9-b51956b4aff3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-73ea5849-0a8b-5581-9a68-493551edf6c6" "answer-1ccc6750-246a-5a6e-8d0b-5e04b2fcec20" "answer-8bf1aa4b-de93-56ed-90a9-5e53ffe6181a" "answer-445f339a-c1d1-5908-9ed9-b51956b4aff3" "answer-c6a557fd-77df-511c-836c-c00052e9bf42"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73ea5849-0a8b-5581-9a68-493551edf6c6" :answer/id #uuid "73ea5849-0a8b-5581-9a68-493551edf6c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(1+2y)}{(3y+1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ccc6750-246a-5a6e-8d0b-5e04b2fcec20" :answer/id #uuid "1ccc6750-246a-5a6e-8d0b-5e04b2fcec20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(1+2y)}{4(3y+1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8bf1aa4b-de93-56ed-90a9-5e53ffe6181a" :answer/id #uuid "8bf1aa4b-de93-56ed-90a9-5e53ffe6181a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2y}{2(3y+1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-445f339a-c1d1-5908-9ed9-b51956b4aff3" :answer/id #uuid "445f339a-c1d1-5908-9ed9-b51956b4aff3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3(1+2y)}{4(3y+1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6a557fd-77df-511c-836c-c00052e9bf42" :answer/id #uuid "c6a557fd-77df-511c-836c-c00052e9bf42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{4(y+3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163340 :tutorial/content "For continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the **conditional expectation of $X$ given $Y=y$** is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X |Y = y] =\\int\\limits_{\\mathbb R} x \\, f_{X|Y}(x|y)\\;\\textrm dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $f_{X|Y}(x|y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y.$ \n\nThis definition is similar to that of $\\textrm{E}[X].$ However, we now use the conditional PDF $f_{X|Y}(x|y)$ instead of the marginal PDF $f_X(x)$ in our integral.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When we select a particular value of $y,$ the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ returns a real number. However, if $y$ is not specified, i.e., $\\textrm{E}[X |Y = y]$ *depends* on $y,$ we have that $\\textrm{E}[X |Y = y]$ gives us a *function* of $y.$\n\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then $\\textrm E[X | Y = y] = \\textrm E[X]$ for all $y$.\n\n- We sometimes use the notation $\\mu_{X|y}$ to denote $E[X | Y = y].$\n\n- We have an analogous definition for $\\textrm E[Y |X = x]\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[Y |X = x] =\\int\\limits_{\\mathbb R} y \\, f_{Y|X}(y|x)\\;\\textrm dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $f_{Y|X}(y|x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163348 :tutorial/content "Let's find the conditional expectation $\\textrm{E}\\big[X \\,|\\, Y=1\\big]$ given that the conditional probability density function $f_{X|Y}(x \\,|\\, 1)$ is given by\n![](images/8b/8b71da7aecae81ed4e8d9dc27f4730d3d0a03d73146b7b899003d3f8787694bc.svg) \n\nApplying the formula for the conditional expectation of $X$ given $Y=y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\cdot 2(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2{x}^{2}-2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{2(2)^{3}}{3}-(2)^{2}]-[\\frac{2(1)^{3}}{3}-(1)^{2}] \\\\  & =[\\frac{16}{3}-4]-[\\frac{2}{3}-1] \\\\  & =[\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{12}{3}]-[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{3}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162193 :activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100825 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-862b92b4-3ae1-5d18-841c-0f1c9c5cb6ec" "question-a12bbd8d-9dfc-5edb-a067-bd2603dd76bf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-862b92b4-3ae1-5d18-841c-0f1c9c5cb6ec" :question/id #uuid "862b92b4-3ae1-5d18-841c-0f1c9c5cb6ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-20254" :question/problem "![](images/42/42e163dd119aaea37d2a373310b408a409023400a6f84ff520c464c6acb153b9.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0.3)$ | $0.3$ | $(0.3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x≈-0.3.$ So ${f}^{″}(-0.3)≈0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${f}^{′}(-0.3)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x≈-0.3,$ the slope is positive.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1b048d1b6c430f023c0419d7c8442740b76b5d7e646f32fc870be9225649b70.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-82ad018d-6532-5573-be38-3543ae240b24"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-82ad018d-6532-5573-be38-3543ae240b24" :answer-field/id #uuid "82ad018d-6532-5573-be38-3543ae240b24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-174b4041-f106-59ec-ab7a-44ef501bae0f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f2b3ace1-61e2-532e-99a7-bee4fa63be58" "answer-e833c9ed-ee14-5418-8a3c-defcce10db9f" "answer-a0ee2a52-7af7-568a-838d-0419c87c99a2" "answer-174b4041-f106-59ec-ab7a-44ef501bae0f" "answer-2a7adb23-6c82-5afe-af17-e71cf492519e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2b3ace1-61e2-532e-99a7-bee4fa63be58" :answer/id #uuid "f2b3ace1-61e2-532e-99a7-bee4fa63be58" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/1a/1a099796621d60952dbd0e26a51222c8d9f29df5c3f8b5f141de759ff00bf825.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e833c9ed-ee14-5418-8a3c-defcce10db9f" :answer/id #uuid "e833c9ed-ee14-5418-8a3c-defcce10db9f" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/3c/3cb013ecb259c79858ae8705e06ddff54e5c0b5c01d11d5047fd62933e93baf2.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0ee2a52-7af7-568a-838d-0419c87c99a2" :answer/id #uuid "a0ee2a52-7af7-568a-838d-0419c87c99a2" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/77/77e6766deafc946e3b985fb3922a080a8ddad7f5be199fd314c6bf33d676149d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-174b4041-f106-59ec-ab7a-44ef501bae0f" :answer/id #uuid "174b4041-f106-59ec-ab7a-44ef501bae0f" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b1/b1b048d1b6c430f023c0419d7c8442740b76b5d7e646f32fc870be9225649b70.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a7adb23-6c82-5afe-af17-e71cf492519e" :answer/id #uuid "2a7adb23-6c82-5afe-af17-e71cf492519e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/ff/ff347ada6a917e7d6da98f1f9ab39b484ac7e50211bbae597b21066a74b9282a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100930 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c5bdd27b-0119-529a-86ed-c9eae1777103"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101483 :question/problem "![](images/17/174178e1d4c527bb81ea3ba726e5ef753b40dd85b452d65faf05590e7a387a32.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function is piecewise-linear, we get a constant derivative over each linear part.\nNotice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is constant, we get ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $\\text{const.}$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $0$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. \n\nAdditionally, we have a change of the slope of the tangent line (from negative to $0$) when we pass through $x=4$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=4$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/87/877fcf017ab91010648de9a375e535ec7697841c8e317196c5759df6aca32e19.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100059 :answer-field/correct 17592186100062 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-a12bbd8d-9dfc-5edb-a067-bd2603dd76bf" :question/id #uuid "a12bbd8d-9dfc-5edb-a067-bd2603dd76bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-114627" :question/problem "![](images/fe/fe369706a9e211bfc5265041bbc5d2ec0fab69eb205c23111f548785a2f88708.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x≈0.$ So ${f}^{″}(0)≈0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${f}^{′}(0)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x≈0,$ the slope is positive.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1f5a0aa83f9348cfae1bf2ef472a00e1da51137d03284bc78eea67d929aaa80e.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4d0b2e54-91a9-512b-93ce-8c8288271db5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4d0b2e54-91a9-512b-93ce-8c8288271db5" :answer-field/id #uuid "4d0b2e54-91a9-512b-93ce-8c8288271db5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2456fff0-2a4b-5357-8c10-186ee0d990c6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2456fff0-2a4b-5357-8c10-186ee0d990c6" "answer-07db90e3-0a59-5db0-b415-ff2cc2acfbb9" "answer-c3305299-f594-5d0f-9ba5-a922f8fdab0e" "answer-33647010-0ca9-5c0e-86d3-15e55a578800" "answer-44735508-9483-5b63-a255-6a13f9fbf3e2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2456fff0-2a4b-5357-8c10-186ee0d990c6" :answer/id #uuid "2456fff0-2a4b-5357-8c10-186ee0d990c6" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/1f/1f5a0aa83f9348cfae1bf2ef472a00e1da51137d03284bc78eea67d929aaa80e.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07db90e3-0a59-5db0-b415-ff2cc2acfbb9" :answer/id #uuid "07db90e3-0a59-5db0-b415-ff2cc2acfbb9" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/21/2120febb62cb220085338423b3677c965ef05de4a03c8e15a003317e787af1e7.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3305299-f594-5d0f-9ba5-a922f8fdab0e" :answer/id #uuid "c3305299-f594-5d0f-9ba5-a922f8fdab0e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/47/47c0127e6829858e84bbf4e89c22b7248ca95f16a6fd967831aedba4b5c142f8.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33647010-0ca9-5c0e-86d3-15e55a578800" :answer/id #uuid "33647010-0ca9-5c0e-86d3-15e55a578800" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/d9/d9a9d1fce2a8e02903fcdd1dc0d83c49fa7ab9c8fbfb3aaf7ccdc6f292accb14.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44735508-9483-5b63-a255-6a13f9fbf3e2" :answer/id #uuid "44735508-9483-5b63-a255-6a13f9fbf3e2" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/e4/e475a76051bdb6f7e30c6b34319d7409afd5cf6af3ffdcfd1d5d07d93d31f58c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c5bdd27b-0119-529a-86ed-c9eae1777103" :question/id #uuid "c5bdd27b-0119-529a-86ed-c9eae1777103" :question/math-academy-id "q-114644" :question/problem "![](images/53/53693867d697a4aaf7bedd2267ac2e77087b01a270f44ce68e9a4d303b81a5ad.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function is piecewise-linear, we get a constant derivative over each linear part.\nNotice the following:\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we get ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $\\text{undef.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points. \n\nAdditionally, we have a change of the slope of the tangent line (from positive to negative)  when we pass through $x=0$ from left to right. This means that the derivative is not defined at $x=0$ and we obtain a jump discontinuity at this point for the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bf3b558331bf2e816cf0df6d756f55686aeb06f6ec9ce496019bd06d14684b32.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9efc5b3d-9312-5242-bda0-ac0535473480"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9efc5b3d-9312-5242-bda0-ac0535473480" :answer-field/id #uuid "9efc5b3d-9312-5242-bda0-ac0535473480" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8fc14647-e2e3-5824-b3c6-c8f100a86208" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5a14fb76-81ef-5b51-a32c-50df3b3cebd2" "answer-a95ac06a-4df1-528f-96b0-e8e1a3d3a5e3" "answer-8fc14647-e2e3-5824-b3c6-c8f100a86208" "answer-cc1513fe-facd-5469-826a-aceea9efe925" "answer-d9ffc8cb-7b92-5d4c-abba-f07a6cdf7a5e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a14fb76-81ef-5b51-a32c-50df3b3cebd2" :answer/id #uuid "5a14fb76-81ef-5b51-a32c-50df3b3cebd2" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/84/84113b8f73965322a975402bbbd40f198036928e40f5865b00b01edb76ba898c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a95ac06a-4df1-528f-96b0-e8e1a3d3a5e3" :answer/id #uuid "a95ac06a-4df1-528f-96b0-e8e1a3d3a5e3" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b6/b6a3eea0bb86d31c655f1108469d35b8091e6f7b2dafca8a5f3b73e316f0cc6e.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fc14647-e2e3-5824-b3c6-c8f100a86208" :answer/id #uuid "8fc14647-e2e3-5824-b3c6-c8f100a86208" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/bf/bf3b558331bf2e816cf0df6d756f55686aeb06f6ec9ce496019bd06d14684b32.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc1513fe-facd-5469-826a-aceea9efe925" :answer/id #uuid "cc1513fe-facd-5469-826a-aceea9efe925" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/cf/cf426dd8f171cf12f1a7f6f9e15c622ebc84fa39511b8163474ece93de77a009.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9ffc8cb-7b92-5d4c-abba-f07a6cdf7a5e" :answer/id #uuid "d9ffc8cb-7b92-5d4c-abba-f07a6cdf7a5e" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/d1/d1d9c79b15d10a5035998809b714b480c87a0c13825aa8aadf0b8956ebbf90c2.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100826 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3aba7b06-41e0-5e22-b2ba-cc3cc44b0081"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3aba7b06-41e0-5e22-b2ba-cc3cc44b0081" :question/id #uuid "3aba7b06-41e0-5e22-b2ba-cc3cc44b0081" :question/math-academy-id "q-114759" :question/problem "![](images/67/67cfcaae560edcf2fda8c69770088113b6035b46c9951f67ef81f13b8ea9615e.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function $y=g(x)$ has inflection points at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5.$ At the inflection points, we have ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ so the tangent to the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5.$ \n\nWe don't know the exact values of ${g}^{′}(-0.5)$ and ${g}^{′}(0.5)$, but from the graph of $y=g(x)$ we find that the slope of the tangents to ${g}^{′}(x)$ at $x≈-0.5$ and $x≈0.5$ are positive and negative, respectively.  \n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7791dde88c3f8500047c0ebeb4553055d76a98c0c46f903816f41c1506d6a6a.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e5af535f-d80a-5c38-9d0e-a84eb6995c15"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e5af535f-d80a-5c38-9d0e-a84eb6995c15" :answer-field/id #uuid "e5af535f-d80a-5c38-9d0e-a84eb6995c15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-822df945-069f-5212-b24c-a7224f617de9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d4e60351-e333-5a6b-be45-1d4de70bae1b" "answer-bfd164fc-a22a-56dc-ae5e-f763c3ff80fa" "answer-c6b0efff-abae-54cb-bfb3-65f66a460a4f" "answer-831caeb4-4e08-551b-af16-8bc85d4372ec" "answer-822df945-069f-5212-b24c-a7224f617de9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4e60351-e333-5a6b-be45-1d4de70bae1b" :answer/id #uuid "d4e60351-e333-5a6b-be45-1d4de70bae1b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/13/13fd6b12240b83c9cffb7fbdaa5d75750b1686ec97a6b4085a6f05dc72c010cc.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfd164fc-a22a-56dc-ae5e-f763c3ff80fa" :answer/id #uuid "bfd164fc-a22a-56dc-ae5e-f763c3ff80fa" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/63/6368b0ec13a8859bcfcbdad59d6053605faffaf1b1d9b2069ad2ff6b22fbf778.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6b0efff-abae-54cb-bfb3-65f66a460a4f" :answer/id #uuid "c6b0efff-abae-54cb-bfb3-65f66a460a4f" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/d8/d847b7acb517b10f70af82cad1054b586cacb245e2389ccfd3296c244dfab6cd.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-831caeb4-4e08-551b-af16-8bc85d4372ec" :answer/id #uuid "831caeb4-4e08-551b-af16-8bc85d4372ec" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/db/db832b68d0acb869dfb1431f3bf55acc1085b25babbdde5b9583eae1da9b49b8.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-822df945-069f-5212-b24c-a7224f617de9" :answer/id #uuid "822df945-069f-5212-b24c-a7224f617de9" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/e7/e7791dde88c3f8500047c0ebeb4553055d76a98c0c46f903816f41c1506d6a6a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-2d4484de-f9fb-54c3-89bf-df23e280c65c" :activity/id #uuid "2d4484de-f9fb-54c3-89bf-df23e280c65c" :activity/title "Sketching the Derivative of a Function From the Function's Graph" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050166 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-2960f11d-8f32-50a8-8883-ed0e4c737260" "step-e2e6d779-f0e8-5097-a067-f233c8dbe5b5" "step-54ea61c8-4bc1-5ef1-9f92-18ea362bb2b9" "step-4b56d5e6-9f66-5459-b308-041c603898f6" "step-522c7806-eb48-5c75-b4c5-3c468ee4a292"] :activity/first-step "step-2960f11d-8f32-50a8-8883-ed0e4c737260" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-2960f11d-8f32-50a8-8883-ed0e4c737260" :step/id #uuid "2960f11d-8f32-50a8-8883-ed0e4c737260" :step/content "question-862b92b4-3ae1-5d18-841c-0f1c9c5cb6ec" :step/next "step-e2e6d779-f0e8-5097-a067-f233c8dbe5b5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e2e6d779-f0e8-5097-a067-f233c8dbe5b5" :step/id #uuid "e2e6d779-f0e8-5097-a067-f233c8dbe5b5" :step/content 17592186101483 :step/next "step-54ea61c8-4bc1-5ef1-9f92-18ea362bb2b9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-54ea61c8-4bc1-5ef1-9f92-18ea362bb2b9" :step/id #uuid "54ea61c8-4bc1-5ef1-9f92-18ea362bb2b9" :step/content "question-a12bbd8d-9dfc-5edb-a067-bd2603dd76bf" :step/next "step-4b56d5e6-9f66-5459-b308-041c603898f6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4b56d5e6-9f66-5459-b308-041c603898f6" :step/id #uuid "4b56d5e6-9f66-5459-b308-041c603898f6" :step/content "question-c5bdd27b-0119-529a-86ed-c9eae1777103" :step/next "step-522c7806-eb48-5c75-b4c5-3c468ee4a292" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-522c7806-eb48-5c75-b4c5-3c468ee4a292" :step/id #uuid "522c7806-eb48-5c75-b4c5-3c468ee4a292" :step/content "question-3aba7b06-41e0-5e22-b2ba-cc3cc44b0081" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186123098 :answer-field/choices 17592186123099] [:db/retract 17592186123098 :answer-field/choices 17592186123100] [:db/retract 17592186123098 :answer-field/choices 17592186123101] [:db/retract 17592186123098 :answer-field/choices 17592186123102] [:db/retract 17592186123098 :answer-field/choices 17592186123103] [:db/retract 17592186123104 :answer-field/choices 17592186123105] [:db/retract 17592186123104 :answer-field/choices 17592186123106] [:db/retract 17592186123104 :answer-field/choices 17592186123107] [:db/retract 17592186123104 :answer-field/choices 17592186123108] [:db/retract 17592186123104 :answer-field/choices 17592186123109] {:db/id 17592186125083 :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+2xy+3{y}^{2}+2x+10y+1,$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy+3{y}^{2}+2x+10y+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy+3{y}^{2}+2x+10y+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(x+y+1) & 2(x+3y+5)\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+1=0 \\\\ x+3y+5=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n2y+4=0\\,⟹\\,y=-2.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx+(-2)+1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,-2)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(x+y+1))=2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(x+3y+5))=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(x+y+1))=2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(x+3y+5))=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-2)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,-2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,-2)=det({f}^{″}(1,-2))=2\\cdot 6-{2}^{2}=8>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,-2)=({f}^{″}(1,-2))_{11}=2>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,-2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186123098 :answer-field/correct "answer-23d7c23e-2e7c-57ed-a669-10ce3af13e38" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fe0c6bef-702a-5967-84e7-559ce04a5279" "answer-4396e4d8-a763-52af-afef-ded9f6e55a26" "answer-1caf69dd-971a-5d35-89cb-d9626dcb78d5" "answer-23d7c23e-2e7c-57ed-a669-10ce3af13e38" "answer-2c29ee3e-35ef-5627-a600-d2e0619b9541"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe0c6bef-702a-5967-84e7-559ce04a5279" :answer/id #uuid "fe0c6bef-702a-5967-84e7-559ce04a5279" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-3,2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4396e4d8-a763-52af-afef-ded9f6e55a26" :answer/id #uuid "4396e4d8-a763-52af-afef-ded9f6e55a26" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(1,-2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1caf69dd-971a-5d35-89cb-d9626dcb78d5" :answer/id #uuid "1caf69dd-971a-5d35-89cb-d9626dcb78d5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-3,2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23d7c23e-2e7c-57ed-a669-10ce3af13e38" :answer/id #uuid "23d7c23e-2e7c-57ed-a669-10ce3af13e38" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,-2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c29ee3e-35ef-5627-a600-d2e0619b9541" :answer/id #uuid "2c29ee3e-35ef-5627-a600-d2e0619b9541" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,-2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125084 :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6y & 3{y}^{2}-6x\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6y & 3{y}^{2}-6x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-6y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-6x.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6y=0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-6y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})}^{2}-6x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-2x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}-8x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{3}-8) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}=0,\\,\\,x=2\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(2,2)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-6y)=6x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-6x)=-6 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-6y)=-6\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-6x)=6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6x & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=0\\cdot 0-(-6)^{2}=-36<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(2,2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,2)=[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(2,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,2)=det({f}^{″}(2,2))=12\\cdot 12-(-6)^{2}=108>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(2,2)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,2)=({f}^{″}(2,2))_{11}=12>0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(2,2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186123104 :answer-field/correct "answer-5e1b31e7-050e-5530-973b-420e7f556047" :answer-field/choices ["answer-da384a82-fb82-524f-a94b-1ea107ebde1d" "answer-a61d9d31-5126-5495-bb34-7b540584d0e1" "answer-114d029d-432f-5d2d-a8e0-da4352b9cbe0" "answer-5e1b31e7-050e-5530-973b-420e7f556047" "answer-8a1bf858-df10-5d12-842f-190930cca500"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da384a82-fb82-524f-a94b-1ea107ebde1d" :answer/id #uuid "da384a82-fb82-524f-a94b-1ea107ebde1d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(-2,-2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a61d9d31-5126-5495-bb34-7b540584d0e1" :answer/id #uuid "a61d9d31-5126-5495-bb34-7b540584d0e1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$ and a saddle point at $(-2,-2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-114d029d-432f-5d2d-a8e0-da4352b9cbe0" :answer/id #uuid "114d029d-432f-5d2d-a8e0-da4352b9cbe0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(2,2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e1b31e7-050e-5530-973b-420e7f556047" :answer/id #uuid "5e1b31e7-050e-5530-973b-420e7f556047" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(2,2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a1bf858-df10-5d12-842f-190930cca500" :answer/id #uuid "8a1bf858-df10-5d12-842f-190930cca500" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(0,0)$ and $(2,2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-60652dd5-fdd9-5974-9d65-a71597afa3f3" "question-f7f93cc0-fc36-5de6-903e-61c89b0db59c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-60652dd5-fdd9-5974-9d65-a71597afa3f3" :question/id #uuid "60652dd5-fdd9-5974-9d65-a71597afa3f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-104994" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+2y-6 & 2x+2y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(3,-3).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(3,-3)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)<0$\n\nIII. The point $(3,-3)$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+2y-6 & 2x+2y\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4x+2y-6,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2x+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+2y-6)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(3,-3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(3,-3)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(3,-3),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(3,-3)=det({f}^{″}(3,-3))=4\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}=4>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)=({f}^{″}(3,-3))_{11}=4>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(3,-3)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)>0,$ the point $(3,-3)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-06c9a2bb-e41c-52c6-8b2c-a83b5dffeaf4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-06c9a2bb-e41c-52c6-8b2c-a83b5dffeaf4" :answer-field/id #uuid "06c9a2bb-e41c-52c6-8b2c-a83b5dffeaf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3bef4c65-0c4b-5059-b575-1485beb2844b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d30d25a2-0e79-557a-a62e-539de549b8be" "answer-3bef4c65-0c4b-5059-b575-1485beb2844b" "answer-2b6b1671-0717-5482-8d52-d0c250e890fe" "answer-862feb99-123c-5810-b1cc-80efe435e8f0" "answer-63cd11be-33ac-578f-99a4-b5fbfef6ba96"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d30d25a2-0e79-557a-a62e-539de549b8be" :answer/id #uuid "d30d25a2-0e79-557a-a62e-539de549b8be" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bef4c65-0c4b-5059-b575-1485beb2844b" :answer/id #uuid "3bef4c65-0c4b-5059-b575-1485beb2844b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b6b1671-0717-5482-8d52-d0c250e890fe" :answer/id #uuid "2b6b1671-0717-5482-8d52-d0c250e890fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-862feb99-123c-5810-b1cc-80efe435e8f0" :answer/id #uuid "862feb99-123c-5810-b1cc-80efe435e8f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63cd11be-33ac-578f-99a4-b5fbfef6ba96" :answer/id #uuid "63cd11be-33ac-578f-99a4-b5fbfef6ba96" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f7f93cc0-fc36-5de6-903e-61c89b0db59c" :question/id #uuid "f7f93cc0-fc36-5de6-903e-61c89b0db59c" :question/math-academy-id "q-105213" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(-1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(-1,1)<0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,1)<0$\n\nIII. The point $(-1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & -\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(-1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(-1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,1)=det({f}^{″}(-1,1))=(-2)^{2}-(-1)^{2}=3>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,1)=({f}^{″}(-1,1))_{11}=-2<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(-1,1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,1)<0,$ the point $(-1,1)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6b0046f1-2976-582a-85ca-24a28e09b21d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6b0046f1-2976-582a-85ca-24a28e09b21d" :answer-field/id #uuid "6b0046f1-2976-582a-85ca-24a28e09b21d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ce672a55-9e57-56eb-a86f-0f1f3507439c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3df5a0e2-1ea8-5fe2-a697-20a5c5faa39f" "answer-29711ca7-b3cc-53ec-bec7-d90a0aeb16e5" "answer-ce672a55-9e57-56eb-a86f-0f1f3507439c" "answer-cc0ee0e0-9a43-5369-b3d7-8e3c43d21d2b" "answer-ec9f7794-bd99-5638-b872-bfdbc7829cf2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3df5a0e2-1ea8-5fe2-a697-20a5c5faa39f" :answer/id #uuid "3df5a0e2-1ea8-5fe2-a697-20a5c5faa39f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29711ca7-b3cc-53ec-bec7-d90a0aeb16e5" :answer/id #uuid "29711ca7-b3cc-53ec-bec7-d90a0aeb16e5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce672a55-9e57-56eb-a86f-0f1f3507439c" :answer/id #uuid "ce672a55-9e57-56eb-a86f-0f1f3507439c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc0ee0e0-9a43-5369-b3d7-8e3c43d21d2b" :answer/id #uuid "cc0ee0e0-9a43-5369-b3d7-8e3c43d21d2b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec9f7794-bd99-5638-b872-bfdbc7829cf2" :answer/id #uuid "ec9f7794-bd99-5638-b872-bfdbc7829cf2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-e57ebb2c-da7f-5557-85ec-a93c9bfb4862" :activity/id #uuid "e57ebb2c-da7f-5557-85ec-a93c9bfb4862" :activity/title "The Second Partial Derivatives Test" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186051048 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-6c650994-2746-52bb-b204-a4b55b445bee" "step-e28c95c3-e780-5712-a484-551662e9b69b" "step-d8b6c8ec-acd4-54ce-8211-2be9c4fc4606" "step-11e5811c-fb05-517a-b6a3-b32833e3131b" "step-eb36864f-9d01-5aaa-9ce4-99ddaa5c782f"] :activity/first-step "step-6c650994-2746-52bb-b204-a4b55b445bee" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-6c650994-2746-52bb-b204-a4b55b445bee" :step/id #uuid "6c650994-2746-52bb-b204-a4b55b445bee" :step/content 17592186125083 :step/next "step-e28c95c3-e780-5712-a484-551662e9b69b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e28c95c3-e780-5712-a484-551662e9b69b" :step/id #uuid "e28c95c3-e780-5712-a484-551662e9b69b" :step/content 17592186125084 :step/next "step-d8b6c8ec-acd4-54ce-8211-2be9c4fc4606" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d8b6c8ec-acd4-54ce-8211-2be9c4fc4606" :step/id #uuid "d8b6c8ec-acd4-54ce-8211-2be9c4fc4606" :step/content 17592186434982 :step/next "step-11e5811c-fb05-517a-b6a3-b32833e3131b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-11e5811c-fb05-517a-b6a3-b32833e3131b" :step/id #uuid "11e5811c-fb05-517a-b6a3-b32833e3131b" :step/content "question-60652dd5-fdd9-5974-9d65-a71597afa3f3" :step/next "step-eb36864f-9d01-5aaa-9ce4-99ddaa5c782f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-eb36864f-9d01-5aaa-9ce4-99ddaa5c782f" :step/id #uuid "eb36864f-9d01-5aaa-9ce4-99ddaa5c782f" :step/content "question-f7f93cc0-fc36-5de6-903e-61c89b0db59c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083172 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f7974a75-c042-5467-9fad-57099eb48349"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186082985 :question/worked-solution "The common logarithm of $a$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a = 10^x$ is equivalent to $\\log{a} = x$\n\nIf we compare $10^3 = 1\\,000$ with $10^x = a,$ we get that $a = 1\\,000$ and $x = 3.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as $\\log{1\\,000} = 3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083173 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e5c7ea95-c351-53b5-8952-0f4a790122f8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186082986 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\log\\left( 10^{7}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The common logarithm of a power of $10$ is the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log\\left(10^x\\right) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x =7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log\\left( 10^{7}\\right) = 7.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083174 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-091b6406-ea9f-5edd-85ff-748cd7849985" "question-d37627d8-2221-5b06-8378-80d838ec2dc0" "question-f698077a-723c-5eb7-8769-9b9cd991b10a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186082987 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\log\\left( 0.1 \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $0.1$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n0.1 = 10^{-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\log\\left( 0.1 \\right) = \\log\\left(10^{-1}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm of a power of $10$ is the power itself:\n\n$$\n\\log\\left(10^x\\right) = x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x = -1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\log\\left( 10^{-1} \\right)= -1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083175 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5a64a8e4-76a1-5b39-9c41-0b568b095115" "question-0714c123-409e-586b-bfd3-01ab5d4541d7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186082988 :question/problem "Rounded to $2$ decimal places, what is the value of $2\\log(7) + 5\\log(5)?$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the required expression to $2$ decimal places using a calculator, rounding the intermediate steps to $5$ decimal places to avoid rounding error. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{log}⁡(7)+5\\operatorname{log}⁡(5) & ≈2(0.845\\,10)+5(0.698\\,97) \\\\  & =1.690\\,20+3.494\\,85 \\\\  & =5.185\\,05 \\\\  & ≈5.19.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186234203 :question/worked-solution "The common logarithm of $a$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={10}^{x}$ is equivalent to $\\operatorname{log}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${10}^{-2}=\\frac{1}{100}$ with ${10}^{x}=a,$ we get that $a=\\frac{1}{100}$ and $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{100})=-2.$" :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-04a0273e-3e34-5845-8894-910743f99cff"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-04a0273e-3e34-5845-8894-910743f99cff" :answer-field/id #uuid "04a0273e-3e34-5845-8894-910743f99cff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f635d2ff-c815-5271-95f7-80002177a122" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ae3500ce-45b9-5f87-93da-ff417d20739d" "answer-f635d2ff-c815-5271-95f7-80002177a122" "answer-b9ac4ee7-f1da-55c7-b937-45bb16de187b" "answer-96bd64a4-a693-5801-a289-d7188f556814" "answer-d197b142-08f6-5694-bdaa-5a735426ca9d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae3500ce-45b9-5f87-93da-ff417d20739d" :answer/id #uuid "ae3500ce-45b9-5f87-93da-ff417d20739d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡(-2)=\\frac{1}{100}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f635d2ff-c815-5271-95f7-80002177a122" :answer/id #uuid "f635d2ff-c815-5271-95f7-80002177a122" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{100})=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9ac4ee7-f1da-55c7-b937-45bb16de187b" :answer/id #uuid "b9ac4ee7-f1da-55c7-b937-45bb16de187b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{100})=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96bd64a4-a693-5801-a289-d7188f556814" :answer/id #uuid "96bd64a4-a693-5801-a289-d7188f556814" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=-100" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d197b142-08f6-5694-bdaa-5a735426ca9d" :answer/id #uuid "d197b142-08f6-5694-bdaa-5a735426ca9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡100=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f7974a75-c042-5467-9fad-57099eb48349" :question/id #uuid "f7974a75-c042-5467-9fad-57099eb48349" :question/math-academy-id "q-66546" :question/problem "What exponential equation is equivalent to the logarithmic equation $\\operatorname{log}⁡a=\\frac{1}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The common logarithm of $a$ is defined as follows:\n\n$\\operatorname{log}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={10}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare $\\operatorname{log}⁡a=\\frac{1}{2}$ with $\\operatorname{log}⁡a=x,$ we get that $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as ${10}^{1/2}=a.$\n\nFinally, since ${10}^{1/2}=\\sqrt{10},$ the equation $\\operatorname{log}⁡a=\\frac{1}{2}$ is equivalent to $\\sqrt{10}=a.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c8203a0f-9d9b-5f31-8cf8-b99f535581ef"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c8203a0f-9d9b-5f31-8cf8-b99f535581ef" :answer-field/id #uuid "c8203a0f-9d9b-5f31-8cf8-b99f535581ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-afd98edf-6e85-5875-8c8f-2d9f37432080" :answer-field/choices ["answer-afd98edf-6e85-5875-8c8f-2d9f37432080" "answer-734a3505-488f-5ba5-9239-344967b390c9" "answer-b2ac3a1f-e268-5637-8d9a-06f8157181fc" "answer-c5edad54-fc0c-5d59-bcb3-b5d7fa508eeb" "answer-a0bd1aec-dd21-5a4f-ba66-229fde497e6d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-afd98edf-6e85-5875-8c8f-2d9f37432080" :answer/id #uuid "afd98edf-6e85-5875-8c8f-2d9f37432080" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}=a" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-734a3505-488f-5ba5-9239-344967b390c9" :answer/id #uuid "734a3505-488f-5ba5-9239-344967b390c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{a}=10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2ac3a1f-e268-5637-8d9a-06f8157181fc" :answer/id #uuid "b2ac3a1f-e268-5637-8d9a-06f8157181fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{10}^{-2}=a" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5edad54-fc0c-5d59-bcb3-b5d7fa508eeb" :answer/id #uuid "c5edad54-fc0c-5d59-bcb3-b5d7fa508eeb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{10}^{2}=a" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0bd1aec-dd21-5a4f-ba66-229fde497e6d" :answer/id #uuid "a0bd1aec-dd21-5a4f-ba66-229fde497e6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{10}^{a}=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e5c7ea95-c351-53b5-8952-0f4a790122f8" :question/id #uuid "e5c7ea95-c351-53b5-8952-0f4a790122f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-108087" :question/problem "Find the value of $2\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{3})-\\operatorname{log}⁡(10t)$ at $t=10.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $t=10$ into the function. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{3})-\\operatorname{log}⁡(10t) & =2\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{3})-\\operatorname{log}⁡(10\\cdot 10) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{3})-\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=3$ and $x=2.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{3})-\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{2})=2\\cdot 3-2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-89a053ca-7dc5-596c-888f-925fbf0bae39"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-89a053ca-7dc5-596c-888f-925fbf0bae39" :answer-field/id #uuid "89a053ca-7dc5-596c-888f-925fbf0bae39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-abb0d127-729c-52ec-899c-f4b419e34c74" :answer-field/choices ["answer-35a1c537-1707-54d3-98ae-af863b9980c7" "answer-36c63ad2-e5ab-5c30-950c-3e5928c6abe8" "answer-97afabea-2893-5176-8db1-49f34160a9b0" "answer-b1e80a2a-2a90-5e03-8332-b82cf6f8f572" "answer-abb0d127-729c-52ec-899c-f4b419e34c74"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35a1c537-1707-54d3-98ae-af863b9980c7" :answer/id #uuid "35a1c537-1707-54d3-98ae-af863b9980c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36c63ad2-e5ab-5c30-950c-3e5928c6abe8" :answer/id #uuid "36c63ad2-e5ab-5c30-950c-3e5928c6abe8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97afabea-2893-5176-8db1-49f34160a9b0" :answer/id #uuid "97afabea-2893-5176-8db1-49f34160a9b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1e80a2a-2a90-5e03-8332-b82cf6f8f572" :answer/id #uuid "b1e80a2a-2a90-5e03-8332-b82cf6f8f572" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-abb0d127-729c-52ec-899c-f4b419e34c74" :answer/id #uuid "abb0d127-729c-52ec-899c-f4b419e34c74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186233673 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}⁡(10t)$ at $t=10.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $t=10$ into the function. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡({t}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}⁡(10t) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}⁡(10\\cdot 10) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=2.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{2})+\\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{2})=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3cf199aa-796e-5079-8b74-0ffa7fc0270f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3cf199aa-796e-5079-8b74-0ffa7fc0270f" :answer-field/id #uuid "3cf199aa-796e-5079-8b74-0ffa7fc0270f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2878795d-549b-59c9-ab12-604845f198fc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4f125697-4b57-5a03-b10b-7c12d3222c65" "answer-94caaf5e-9961-580b-a4ff-185ea4d14ee6" "answer-ef5b568b-c49c-5a58-bd84-6e27e1eaa6f5" "answer-ac82dbfa-1a2b-5f4d-84b4-dbc88643360a" "answer-2878795d-549b-59c9-ab12-604845f198fc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f125697-4b57-5a03-b10b-7c12d