wer/id #uuid "d7b976e5-3d11-5eb8-b26a-10408be52ab8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "m=\\sqrt{3},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2a147b6b-b927-5c0b-b768-701655ad01fc" :question/id #uuid "2a147b6b-b927-5c0b-b768-701655ad01fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-114119" :question/problem "Find all of the real solutions to the equation ${x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+3x-15=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+3x-15 & = \\\\ ({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2})+(3x-15) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-5)+3(x-5) & = \\\\ (x-5)\\cdot {x}^{2}+(x-5)\\cdot 3 & = \\\\ (x-5)({x}^{2}+3) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(x-5)({x}^{2}+3)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(x-5)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5 & =0 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({x}^{2}+3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+3) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince a squared real number cannot be negative, the equation ${x}^{2}+3=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore,  $x=5$ is the only real solution." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6d8ec166-959b-5586-85f7-387a034ebd24"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6d8ec166-959b-5586-85f7-387a034ebd24" :answer-field/id #uuid "6d8ec166-959b-5586-85f7-387a034ebd24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f13bd088-a5c4-52b8-8763-a1805c6f88af" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f13bd088-a5c4-52b8-8763-a1805c6f88af" "answer-7804cb00-dc3d-59ca-985c-d750e65bdcfd" "answer-e32bec55-bb10-5bc2-9fe9-c78513ac1c44" "answer-5b2afd65-6421-5c85-809b-f3d3bbc23063" "answer-c3f3d448-e53c-5baf-a19e-86cd0118bed6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f13bd088-a5c4-52b8-8763-a1805c6f88af" :answer/id #uuid "f13bd088-a5c4-52b8-8763-a1805c6f88af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7804cb00-dc3d-59ca-985c-d750e65bdcfd" :answer/id #uuid "7804cb00-dc3d-59ca-985c-d750e65bdcfd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=5,-\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e32bec55-bb10-5bc2-9fe9-c78513ac1c44" :answer/id #uuid "e32bec55-bb10-5bc2-9fe9-c78513ac1c44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=5\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b2afd65-6421-5c85-809b-f3d3bbc23063" :answer/id #uuid "5b2afd65-6421-5c85-809b-f3d3bbc23063" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\pm 5,\\pm \\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3f3d448-e53c-5baf-a19e-86cd0118bed6" :answer/id #uuid "c3f3d448-e53c-5baf-a19e-86cd0118bed6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bb562da5-ef3b-592a-bfc0-4d26f88b1f50" :question/id #uuid "bb562da5-ef3b-592a-bfc0-4d26f88b1f50" :question/math-academy-id "q-114198" :question/problem "Solve $36{d}^{3}-81{d}^{2}-16d+36=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed to factor the left side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{d}^{3}-81{d}^{2}-16d+36 & = \\\\ (36{d}^{3}-81{d}^{2})-(16d-36) & = \\\\ 9{d}^{2}(4d-9)-4(4d-9) & = \\\\ (4d-9)\\cdot 9{d}^{2}-(4d-9)\\cdot 4 & = \\\\ (4d-9)(9{d}^{2}-4) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(4d-9)(9{d}^{2}-4)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(4d-9)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4d-9 & =0 \\\\ 4d & =9 \\\\ d & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(9{d}^{2}-4)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{d}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 9{d}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {d}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ d & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $d=\\frac{9}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cd3ee59c-b21b-5bdc-b880-b22cc5016b54"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cd3ee59c-b21b-5bdc-b880-b22cc5016b54" :answer-field/id #uuid "cd3ee59c-b21b-5bdc-b880-b22cc5016b54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fa362661-1528-514a-98ce-2bf9cde68db6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-556dab46-acc8-5752-9b58-7065d4927d67" "answer-fa362661-1528-514a-98ce-2bf9cde68db6" "answer-05e895a8-20d5-5be3-91e6-fea5ef55ae13" "answer-f970778b-b4ef-590e-9c25-ab9706ce2474" "answer-b5bc49b7-a136-5f0a-ba95-6eeee343a589"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-556dab46-acc8-5752-9b58-7065d4927d67" :answer/id #uuid "556dab46-acc8-5752-9b58-7065d4927d67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "d=\\pm \\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa362661-1528-514a-98ce-2bf9cde68db6" :answer/id #uuid "fa362661-1528-514a-98ce-2bf9cde68db6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "d=\\pm \\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{9}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05e895a8-20d5-5be3-91e6-fea5ef55ae13" :answer/id #uuid "05e895a8-20d5-5be3-91e6-fea5ef55ae13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "d=\\pm \\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f970778b-b4ef-590e-9c25-ab9706ce2474" :answer/id #uuid "f970778b-b4ef-590e-9c25-ab9706ce2474" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "d=\\pm \\frac{4}{9},\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5bc49b7-a136-5f0a-ba95-6eeee343a589" :answer/id #uuid "b5bc49b7-a136-5f0a-ba95-6eeee343a589" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "d=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}},\\,\\frac{7}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083532 :question/problem "Solve $12{y}^{3}+10{y}^{2}-6y-5=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed to factor the left side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{y}^{3}+10{y}^{2}-6y-5 & = \\\\ (12{y}^{3}+10{y}^{2})-(6y+5) & = \\\\ 2{y}^{2}(6y+5)-(6y+5) & = \\\\ (6y+5)\\cdot 2{y}^{2}-(6y+5)\\cdot 1 & = \\\\ (6y+5)(2{y}^{2}-1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(6y+5)(2{y}^{2}-1)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(6y+5)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y+5 & =0 \\\\ 6y & =-5 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(2{y}^{2}-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=-\\frac{5}{6},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186080087 :answer-field/correct "answer-ccd8eb40-31a9-59f6-af8c-d8c1138512bb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bac08756-aaf5-558b-bead-64d9e3ef70b0" "answer-1740ebf8-34bc-5bef-9983-7a65b38610cd" "answer-e114765f-9781-5d1d-aec3-c40d54965149" "answer-ccd8eb40-31a9-59f6-af8c-d8c1138512bb" "answer-b88d6d9a-7314-5259-9e8f-d8e3250cd16b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bac08756-aaf5-558b-bead-64d9e3ef70b0" :answer/id #uuid "bac08756-aaf5-558b-bead-64d9e3ef70b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1740ebf8-34bc-5bef-9983-7a65b38610cd" :answer/id #uuid "1740ebf8-34bc-5bef-9983-7a65b38610cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{4}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e114765f-9781-5d1d-aec3-c40d54965149" :answer/id #uuid "e114765f-9781-5d1d-aec3-c40d54965149" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{4}{3},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{6}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccd8eb40-31a9-59f6-af8c-d8c1138512bb" :answer/id #uuid "ccd8eb40-31a9-59f6-af8c-d8c1138512bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{6},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b88d6d9a-7314-5259-9e8f-d8e3250cd16b" :answer/id #uuid "b88d6d9a-7314-5259-9e8f-d8e3250cd16b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bbddec8f-0415-5a7f-a473-3ac96a89f653" :question/id #uuid "bbddec8f-0415-5a7f-a473-3ac96a89f653" :question/math-academy-id "q-114207" :question/problem "Solve $-2{r}^{3}+12{r}^{2}+3r-18=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$ to make the coefficient of the cubic term positive. This gives\n\n$$\n2{r}^{3}-12{r}^{2}-3r+18=0.\n$$\n\nWe then proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{r}^{3}-12{r}^{2}-3r+18 & = \\\\ (2{r}^{3}-12{r}^{2})+(-3r+18) & = \\\\ 2{r}^{2}(r-6)-3(r-6) & = \\\\ (2{r}^{2}-3)(r-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(2{r}^{2}-3)(r-6)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(2{r}^{2}-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get $r=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(r-6)$ equal to $0,$ we get $r=6.$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $r=6,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5cd45dfb-9cad-5c3d-946b-21eb406e1d7e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5cd45dfb-9cad-5c3d-946b-21eb406e1d7e" :answer-field/id #uuid "5cd45dfb-9cad-5c3d-946b-21eb406e1d7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b98f9771-5de9-571d-96fe-04304b611ff7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ffa87e20-7120-5b08-8f33-95132a4dcbe6" "answer-dcb2caea-0ad4-571d-987f-a03f34b6b81d" "answer-bc94728e-0409-57f5-a0a4-2df3910b17c4" "answer-b98f9771-5de9-571d-96fe-04304b611ff7" "answer-35e4f392-48f5-541b-aa88-739a0fba7056"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffa87e20-7120-5b08-8f33-95132a4dcbe6" :answer/id #uuid "ffa87e20-7120-5b08-8f33-95132a4dcbe6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=2,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcb2caea-0ad4-571d-987f-a03f34b6b81d" :answer/id #uuid "dcb2caea-0ad4-571d-987f-a03f34b6b81d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=6,\\,\\pm 2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc94728e-0409-57f5-a0a4-2df3910b17c4" :answer/id #uuid "bc94728e-0409-57f5-a0a4-2df3910b17c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=6,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b98f9771-5de9-571d-96fe-04304b611ff7" :answer/id #uuid "b98f9771-5de9-571d-96fe-04304b611ff7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=6,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35e4f392-48f5-541b-aa88-739a0fba7056" :answer/id #uuid "35e4f392-48f5-541b-aa88-739a0fba7056" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083678 :question/problem "Solve $-9{y}^{3}+27{y}^{2}+y-3=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$ to make the coefficient of the cubic term positive. This gives\n\n$$\n9{y}^{3}-27{y}^{2}-y+3=0.\n$$\n\nWe then proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{3}-27{y}^{2}-y+3 & = \\\\ 9{y}^{2}(y-3)-1(y-3) & = \\\\ (9{y}^{2}-1)(y-3) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(9{y}^{2}-1)(y-3)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(9{y}^{2}-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(y-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=3,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186080999 :answer-field/correct "answer-116b1441-acff-520b-b6d3-f42fcecb5a41" :answer-field/choices ["answer-20f7df8b-43e3-5960-bc85-50a52c9e641d" "answer-b25a2a64-4e58-5e33-b17b-195d8dc2c1ba" "answer-3e24ec34-1b9f-51ce-9337-9d967ead46f8" "answer-116b1441-acff-520b-b6d3-f42fcecb5a41" "answer-70e32de9-3d4d-5c74-99c2-fe06d0b87974"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20f7df8b-43e3-5960-bc85-50a52c9e641d" :answer/id #uuid "20f7df8b-43e3-5960-bc85-50a52c9e641d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-3,\\pm \\frac{1}{27}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b25a2a64-4e58-5e33-b17b-195d8dc2c1ba" :answer/id #uuid "b25a2a64-4e58-5e33-b17b-195d8dc2c1ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{30}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e24ec34-1b9f-51ce-9337-9d967ead46f8" :answer/id #uuid "3e24ec34-1b9f-51ce-9337-9d967ead46f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3,\\pm \\frac{1}{18}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-116b1441-acff-520b-b6d3-f42fcecb5a41" :answer/id #uuid "116b1441-acff-520b-b6d3-f42fcecb5a41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3,\\pm \\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70e32de9-3d4d-5c74-99c2-fe06d0b87974" :answer/id #uuid "70e32de9-3d4d-5c74-99c2-fe06d0b87974" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3,\\pm \\frac{1}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-faa06da2-05be-56dd-9e44-883b8d4527f9" :question/id #uuid "faa06da2-05be-56dd-9e44-883b8d4527f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-14100" :question/problem "Solve $20{y}^{3}+10{y}^{2}-32y-16=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we have an overall common factor of $2$, we start by dividing our equation through by $2.$ This gives \n\n$$\n10{y}^{3}+5{y}^{2}-16y-8=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{y}^{3}+5{y}^{2}-16y-8 & = \\\\ (10{y}^{3}+5{y}^{2})-(16y+8) & = \\\\ 5{y}^{2}(2y+1)-8(2y+1) & = \\\\ (2y+1)\\cdot 5{y}^{2}-(2y+1)\\cdot 8 & = \\\\ (2y+1)(5{y}^{2}-8) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve:\n\n- Setting the factor $(2y+1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(5{y}^{2}-8)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ 5{y}^{2} & =8 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions to the given equation are $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{5}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2b7ad001-c3eb-5074-a70e-9b8e370cf6ce"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2b7ad001-c3eb-5074-a70e-9b8e370cf6ce" :answer-field/id #uuid "2b7ad001-c3eb-5074-a70e-9b8e370cf6ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-068e3f3b-7c3f-5eb4-b735-b11b0f301183" :answer-field/choices ["answer-068e3f3b-7c3f-5eb4-b735-b11b0f301183" "answer-142c59d9-de17-5701-9761-3e8d234e26e0" "answer-b9e8b378-709c-5af7-a9ce-661162d36425" "answer-7aa263aa-83c7-5421-baaf-8d32501fff76" "answer-fd419487-716f-55d1-984d-658897d900d8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-068e3f3b-7c3f-5eb4-b735-b11b0f301183" :answer/id #uuid "068e3f3b-7c3f-5eb4-b735-b11b0f301183" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{5}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-142c59d9-de17-5701-9761-3e8d234e26e0" :answer/id #uuid "142c59d9-de17-5701-9761-3e8d234e26e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{4}{7},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{5}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9e8b378-709c-5af7-a9ce-661162d36425" :answer/id #uuid "b9e8b378-709c-5af7-a9ce-661162d36425" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-\\frac{8}{5},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{7}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7aa263aa-83c7-5421-baaf-8d32501fff76" :answer/id #uuid "7aa263aa-83c7-5421-baaf-8d32501fff76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=5,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd419487-716f-55d1-984d-658897d900d8" :answer/id #uuid "fd419487-716f-55d1-984d-658897d900d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186233212 :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-888e6ecc-ed2b-57e9-9b31-50cf1eb43d57"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-888e6ecc-ed2b-57e9-9b31-50cf1eb43d57" :answer-field/id #uuid "888e6ecc-ed2b-57e9-9b31-50cf1eb43d57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2a4ef76a-ccfb-5791-b25f-9fe6b8c27ea9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3dda4960-6f22-50d0-9a1b-0aa60ac485af" "answer-f2b17d87-d9ba-516d-a315-6c82fb45de3d" "answer-79fdb319-1ee7-52a8-9cfa-8b3ba59ff030" "answer-2a4f5de1-9dc9-522b-8d92-8e06bd8036e8" "answer-2a4ef76a-ccfb-5791-b25f-9fe6b8c27ea9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3dda4960-6f22-50d0-9a1b-0aa60ac485af" :answer/id #uuid "3dda4960-6f22-50d0-9a1b-0aa60ac485af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u=-4,\\,\\pm 3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2b17d87-d9ba-516d-a315-6c82fb45de3d" :answer/id #uuid "f2b17d87-d9ba-516d-a315-6c82fb45de3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79fdb319-1ee7-52a8-9cfa-8b3ba59ff030" :answer/id #uuid "79fdb319-1ee7-52a8-9cfa-8b3ba59ff030" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u=\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a4f5de1-9dc9-522b-8d92-8e06bd8036e8" :answer/id #uuid "2a4f5de1-9dc9-522b-8d92-8e06bd8036e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u=\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm 4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a4ef76a-ccfb-5791-b25f-9fe6b8c27ea9" :answer/id #uuid "2a4ef76a-ccfb-5791-b25f-9fe6b8c27ea9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u=\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\pm 3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f6abbc6d-e05c-5882-80a0-628ebefcab1b" :question/id #uuid "f6abbc6d-e05c-5882-80a0-628ebefcab1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-114373" :question/problem "Solve $30{n}^{3}+15{n}^{2}-20n-10=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we have an overall common factor of $5$, we start by dividing our equation through by $5.$ This gives \n\n$$\n6{n}^{3}+3{n}^{2}-4n-2=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{n}^{3}+3{n}^{2}-4n-2 & = \\\\ (6{n}^{3}+3{n}^{2})-(4n+2) & = \\\\ 3{n}^{2}(2n+1)-2(2n+1) & = \\\\ (2n+1)\\cdot 3{n}^{2}-(2n+1)\\cdot 2 & = \\\\ (2n+1)(3{n}^{2}-2) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve:\n\n- Setting the factor $(2n+1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2n+1 & =0 \\\\ n & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(3{n}^{2}-2)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{n}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ 3{n}^{2} & =2 \\\\ n & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions to the given equation are $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-88d0ed58-af5e-5cd7-94e2-e1f54c7e910c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-88d0ed58-af5e-5cd7-94e2-e1f54c7e910c" :answer-field/id #uuid "88d0ed58-af5e-5cd7-94e2-e1f54c7e910c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dd9feb0c-7958-5d03-9f8b-fa8015c33036" :answer-field/choices ["answer-45792a27-0fef-5d8f-9ec8-55a9eca333d4" "answer-dd9feb0c-7958-5d03-9f8b-fa8015c33036" "answer-dc1bc403-f223-53d5-a17e-e48db6c9c1bb" "answer-e7778593-47ea-5dd1-bfcc-2dbe06ab0ef4" "answer-ecab74bd-d166-5e48-97af-29ff8b13f687"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45792a27-0fef-5d8f-9ec8-55a9eca333d4" :answer/id #uuid "45792a27-0fef-5d8f-9ec8-55a9eca333d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "n=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd9feb0c-7958-5d03-9f8b-fa8015c33036" :answer/id #uuid "dd9feb0c-7958-5d03-9f8b-fa8015c33036" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "n=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc1bc403-f223-53d5-a17e-e48db6c9c1bb" :answer/id #uuid "dc1bc403-f223-53d5-a17e-e48db6c9c1bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "n=-\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7778593-47ea-5dd1-bfcc-2dbe06ab0ef4" :answer/id #uuid "e7778593-47ea-5dd1-bfcc-2dbe06ab0ef4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "n=-\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecab74bd-d166-5e48-97af-29ff8b13f687" :answer/id #uuid "ecab74bd-d166-5e48-97af-29ff8b13f687" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "n=-\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm 1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3e7651d9-4306-5281-ab2c-60e5fbcc4dde" :question/id #uuid "3e7651d9-4306-5281-ab2c-60e5fbcc4dde" :question/math-academy-id "q-114335" :question/problem "Solve $3{t}^{3}-15{t}^{2}-9t+45=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we have an overall common factor of $3$, we start by dividing our equation through by $3.$ This gives \n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2}-3t+15=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3}-5{t}^{2}-3t+15 & = \\\\ ({t}^{3}-5{t}^{2})-(3t-15) & = \\\\ {t}^{2}(t-5)-3(t-5) & = \\\\ (t-5)\\cdot {t}^{2}-(t-5)\\cdot 3 & = \\\\ (t-5)({t}^{2}-3) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve:\n\n- Setting the factor $(t-5)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-5 & =0 \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({t}^{2}-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =3 \\\\ t & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions to the given equation are $5$ and $\\pm \\sqrt{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ea6d0dca-1729-5bc6-9b6f-8503ef6c372f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ea6d0dca-1729-5bc6-9b6f-8503ef6c372f" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea6d0dca-1729-5bc6-9b6f-8503ef6c372f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-34f7cfa1-4e93-5517-b155-e270eff09e80" :answer-field/choices ["answer-88dfdb34-3660-5235-afc7-fde23954559d" "answer-308cd250-5d9c-5d92-99a8-06286d6838ec" "answer-fb90974b-a2c6-5b1d-a585-3e248c3e216d" "answer-34f7cfa1-4e93-5517-b155-e270eff09e80" "answer-c81ac854-29ab-5769-b7c7-d1a8b87a650d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88dfdb34-3660-5235-afc7-fde23954559d" :answer/id #uuid "88dfdb34-3660-5235-afc7-fde23954559d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=-2,\\,3,\\,5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-308cd250-5d9c-5d92-99a8-06286d6838ec" :answer/id #uuid "308cd250-5d9c-5d92-99a8-06286d6838ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb90974b-a2c6-5b1d-a585-3e248c3e216d" :answer/id #uuid "fb90974b-a2c6-5b1d-a585-3e248c3e216d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=\\pm 3,\\,5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34f7cfa1-4e93-5517-b155-e270eff09e80" :answer/id #uuid "34f7cfa1-4e93-5517-b155-e270eff09e80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=\\pm \\sqrt{3},\\,5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c81ac854-29ab-5769-b7c7-d1a8b87a650d" :answer/id #uuid "c81ac854-29ab-5769-b7c7-d1a8b87a650d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=\\sqrt{3},\\,5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186084455 :tutorial/content "We have learned how to solve quadratic equations by factoring. Just like quadratic equations, some (but not all) cubic equations can also be solved by factoring.\n\nTo solve a cubic equation by factoring, we just need to follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Write the equation with $0$ on one side\n\n2\\. Factor the expression on the other side\n\n3\\. Set each factor equal to $0$ and solve\n\nTo factor the cubic, we can often use the method of grouping.\n\n**Note:** The cubic equations in the following examples can all be factored, but keep in mind that most cubic equations cannot be!" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083422 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096843 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-321f22b7-0ca5-594c-82a7-c5cf5cf23fad" "question-73597027-954f-5bff-8c64-de058a3fe822" "question-930f9486-b8c5-5ccf-b917-a4806acde6bf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-321f22b7-0ca5-594c-82a7-c5cf5cf23fad" :question/id #uuid "321f22b7-0ca5-594c-82a7-c5cf5cf23fad" :question/math-academy-id "q-71952" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-2x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)={x}^{3}-2x$ and $f(g(x))=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{g}}=2{g}^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =2({g}^{-1/2})^{′}=-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3}-2x)^{′}=3{x}^{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}-2x)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-686cff4f-8265-5ba3-8560-bcdf5671d8d5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-686cff4f-8265-5ba3-8560-bcdf5671d8d5" :answer-field/id #uuid "686cff4f-8265-5ba3-8560-bcdf5671d8d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0e0c3af3-091e-560d-ac8e-abe55cc9d0f5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-86d89cd3-8e55-5f29-83ed-c409f606d553" "answer-0e0c3af3-091e-560d-ac8e-abe55cc9d0f5" "answer-996b8fd1-b20a-565b-98d1-c22e6ee7d68a" "answer-75d7df1e-163d-51cf-9003-0d07b95639d4" "answer-0bd0f410-6e9b-5a13-84b6-2793313eb013"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86d89cd3-8e55-5f29-83ed-c409f606d553" :answer/id #uuid "86d89cd3-8e55-5f29-83ed-c409f606d553" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-2x}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e0c3af3-091e-560d-ac8e-abe55cc9d0f5" :answer/id #uuid "0e0c3af3-091e-560d-ac8e-abe55cc9d0f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}-2x)^{3}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-996b8fd1-b20a-565b-98d1-c22e6ee7d68a" :answer/id #uuid "996b8fd1-b20a-565b-98d1-c22e6ee7d68a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}+3}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}-2x)^{3}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75d7df1e-163d-51cf-9003-0d07b95639d4" :answer/id #uuid "75d7df1e-163d-51cf-9003-0d07b95639d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-2x}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0bd0f410-6e9b-5a13-84b6-2793313eb013" :answer/id #uuid "0bd0f410-6e9b-5a13-84b6-2793313eb013" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}-3}{\\sqrt{({x}^{3}-2x)^{3}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-73597027-954f-5bff-8c64-de058a3fe822" :question/id #uuid "73597027-954f-5bff-8c64-de058a3fe822" :question/math-academy-id "q-107296" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(1-3x)}^{8},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=1-3x$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{8}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{8})^{′}=8{g}^{7}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(1-3x)^{′}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =8{g}^{7}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-24(1-3x)^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-44da7608-9cb4-57b2-9694-53ee352f7833"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-44da7608-9cb4-57b2-9694-53ee352f7833" :answer-field/id #uuid "44da7608-9cb4-57b2-9694-53ee352f7833" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-99709777-eb56-5d41-ac58-1c43410ac856" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4793072d-d61e-5f93-bb0c-993627ee72c2" "answer-99709777-eb56-5d41-ac58-1c43410ac856" "answer-e7514498-fddf-54d8-9478-ff10ceb72766" "answer-d2298536-2087-59b8-a435-384b956031dd" "answer-1bf96aed-c4c2-50a0-9312-269557ced95f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4793072d-d61e-5f93-bb0c-993627ee72c2" :answer/id #uuid "4793072d-d61e-5f93-bb0c-993627ee72c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-24(1+3x)^{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99709777-eb56-5d41-ac58-1c43410ac856" :answer/id #uuid "99709777-eb56-5d41-ac58-1c43410ac856" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-24(1-3x)^{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7514498-fddf-54d8-9478-ff10ceb72766" :answer/id #uuid "e7514498-fddf-54d8-9478-ff10ceb72766" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3(1-3x)^{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2298536-2087-59b8-a435-384b956031dd" :answer/id #uuid "d2298536-2087-59b8-a435-384b956031dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24(1+3x)^{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1bf96aed-c4c2-50a0-9312-269557ced95f" :answer/id #uuid "1bf96aed-c4c2-50a0-9312-269557ced95f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8(1-3x)^{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096842 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-98cde390-f268-5ffd-9f2f-31f016a0c432" "question-fa666076-05a3-50c7-be3d-b7a39d6d4f55"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-98cde390-f268-5ffd-9f2f-31f016a0c432" :question/id #uuid "98cde390-f268-5ffd-9f2f-31f016a0c432" :question/math-academy-id "q-107303" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{2x-1},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x-1.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7e463a5a-0bf7-5c15-8a84-e8fb513aab4c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7e463a5a-0bf7-5c15-8a84-e8fb513aab4c" :answer-field/id #uuid "7e463a5a-0bf7-5c15-8a84-e8fb513aab4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-454a250f-2e0e-5eff-ad08-2e1a4569c3b6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-edcc6c31-a212-5340-8bdb-e1b0f8c22746" "answer-a63c2527-a87a-5317-bb52-435b49980a68" "answer-4cf36f6a-ac30-5d76-9499-88f79530ef58" "answer-ceae46e6-5020-51b1-a35e-422b64ddda99" "answer-454a250f-2e0e-5eff-ad08-2e1a4569c3b6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-edcc6c31-a212-5340-8bdb-e1b0f8c22746" :answer/id #uuid "edcc6c31-a212-5340-8bdb-e1b0f8c22746" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a63c2527-a87a-5317-bb52-435b49980a68" :answer/id #uuid "a63c2527-a87a-5317-bb52-435b49980a68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4cf36f6a-ac30-5d76-9499-88f79530ef58" :answer/id #uuid "4cf36f6a-ac30-5d76-9499-88f79530ef58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ceae46e6-5020-51b1-a35e-422b64ddda99" :answer/id #uuid "ceae46e6-5020-51b1-a35e-422b64ddda99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-454a250f-2e0e-5eff-ad08-2e1a4569c3b6" :answer/id #uuid "454a250f-2e0e-5eff-ad08-2e1a4569c3b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fa666076-05a3-50c7-be3d-b7a39d6d4f55" :question/id #uuid "fa666076-05a3-50c7-be3d-b7a39d6d4f55" :question/math-academy-id "q-314192" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x+1}$ at the point where $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=4x+1.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4x+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4x+1}})|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4(0)+1}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3b694189-fec1-5a06-8456-a524e215acf6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3b694189-fec1-5a06-8456-a524e215acf6" :answer-field/id #uuid "3b694189-fec1-5a06-8456-a524e215acf6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-33c7c493-bcdb-5703-aa16-7951eec23ed2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-33c7c493-bcdb-5703-aa16-7951eec23ed2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33c7c493-bcdb-5703-aa16-7951eec23ed2" :answer/id #uuid "33c7c493-bcdb-5703-aa16-7951eec23ed2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-930f9486-b8c5-5ccf-b917-a4806acde6bf" :question/id #uuid "930f9486-b8c5-5ccf-b917-a4806acde6bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-314207" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})}^{2},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=2({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})^{′}=4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot (4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})(\\,4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8b7fd03c-605a-5e8e-8867-8d3e258ab8cf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8b7fd03c-605a-5e8e-8867-8d3e258ab8cf" :answer-field/id #uuid "8b7fd03c-605a-5e8e-8867-8d3e258ab8cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6e10ad36-36ec-5632-8f07-eff850b3becf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6e10ad36-36ec-5632-8f07-eff850b3becf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e10ad36-36ec-5632-8f07-eff850b3becf" :answer/id #uuid "6e10ad36-36ec-5632-8f07-eff850b3becf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4x^{3}-9x^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-63195741-23b2-5a66-a987-8d1b10be7716" :activity/id #uuid "63195741-23b2-5a66-a987-8d1b10be7716" :activity/title "The Chain Rule for Differentiation" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186051084 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-dcb12c9b-27b2-535b-bf95-11fa19d50067" "step-4350c72e-f9bd-5210-90fd-d37e9cc293c0" "step-828caf19-ec46-5c3a-829e-707d043329c0" "step-51f59503-df73-54cc-af64-3dcb163fe7d6" "step-35b3efb8-a2d4-59c0-b538-ee4b244189e0"] :activity/first-step "step-dcb12c9b-27b2-535b-bf95-11fa19d50067" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-dcb12c9b-27b2-535b-bf95-11fa19d50067" :step/id #uuid "dcb12c9b-27b2-535b-bf95-11fa19d50067" :step/content "question-321f22b7-0ca5-594c-82a7-c5cf5cf23fad" :step/next "step-4350c72e-f9bd-5210-90fd-d37e9cc293c0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4350c72e-f9bd-5210-90fd-d37e9cc293c0" :step/id #uuid "4350c72e-f9bd-5210-90fd-d37e9cc293c0" :step/content "question-73597027-954f-5bff-8c64-de058a3fe822" :step/next "step-828caf19-ec46-5c3a-829e-707d043329c0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-828caf19-ec46-5c3a-829e-707d043329c0" :step/id #uuid "828caf19-ec46-5c3a-829e-707d043329c0" :step/content "question-98cde390-f268-5ffd-9f2f-31f016a0c432" :step/next "step-51f59503-df73-54cc-af64-3dcb163fe7d6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-51f59503-df73-54cc-af64-3dcb163fe7d6" :step/id #uuid "51f59503-df73-54cc-af64-3dcb163fe7d6" :step/content "question-fa666076-05a3-50c7-be3d-b7a39d6d4f55" :step/next "step-35b3efb8-a2d4-59c0-b538-ee4b244189e0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-35b3efb8-a2d4-59c0-b538-ee4b244189e0" :step/id #uuid "35b3efb8-a2d4-59c0-b538-ee4b244189e0" :step/content "question-930f9486-b8c5-5ccf-b917-a4806acde6bf" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186150161 :answer-field/choices 17592186150162] [:db/retract 17592186150161 :answer-field/choices 17592186150163] [:db/retract 17592186150161 :answer-field/choices 17592186150164] [:db/retract 17592186150161 :answer-field/choices 17592186150165] [:db/retract 17592186150161 :answer-field/choices 17592186150166] {:db/id 17592186152241 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-817c5709-21ea-5c46-9e06-c595f5917c73"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152609 :question/problem "![](images/81/815e7943ff4f01bf9a89cd6bb1214cf9266c8ae528cfd53e05a7ac56cf1e0e74.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a direct variation between the variables $y$ and $x.$ Which equation describes the relationship between the variables?" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies directly with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=kx.\n$$\n\nNext, we notice there is a point on the graph with $y=8$ and $x=2.$ So, we substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =k\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\frac{8}{2} & =k \\\\ 4 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\ny=4x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186150161 :answer-field/correct "answer-f98ff76b-d478-5a9c-a544-9213463eb301" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a9466f4c-419a-5b13-82db-f0fde5671554" "answer-f98ff76b-d478-5a9c-a544-9213463eb301" "answer-e5402f53-c6f1-5c01-821d-49754b58179c" "answer-cf4e5346-6676-5c14-868d-660ee8405b0d" "answer-4acc6262-ca31-5c5f-ac0d-1ae4e682b14c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9466f4c-419a-5b13-82db-f0fde5671554" :answer/id #uuid "a9466f4c-419a-5b13-82db-f0fde5671554" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f98ff76b-d478-5a9c-a544-9213463eb301" :answer/id #uuid "f98ff76b-d478-5a9c-a544-9213463eb301" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=4x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5402f53-c6f1-5c01-821d-49754b58179c" :answer/id #uuid "e5402f53-c6f1-5c01-821d-49754b58179c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=8x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf4e5346-6676-5c14-868d-660ee8405b0d" :answer/id #uuid "cf4e5346-6676-5c14-868d-660ee8405b0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4acc6262-ca31-5c5f-ac0d-1ae4e682b14c" :answer/id #uuid "4acc6262-ca31-5c5f-ac0d-1ae4e682b14c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-817c5709-21ea-5c46-9e06-c595f5917c73" :question/id #uuid "817c5709-21ea-5c46-9e06-c595f5917c73" :question/math-academy-id "q-68204" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1a5313995e9740a61b455a9c368aca6223a80c8bbea659f06ec2a63c8260747b.png)\n\nKaren decides to walk from her workplace to her house. There is a proportional relationship between the distance $x$ (in kilometers) she covers and the time $y$ (in hours) it takes her to cover that distance.\n\nWhat is the equation for the relationship between $x$ and $y$ shown in the graph above?" :question/worked-solution "Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(3,1).$\n\n![](images/be/bec01f9dc01b340075f09dd459571fe8199116f18a6ab912eb958c7a6f9ee4f2.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{3}x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-df70821b-247b-5776-8c45-aa8d80611751"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-df70821b-247b-5776-8c45-aa8d80611751" :answer-field/id #uuid "df70821b-247b-5776-8c45-aa8d80611751" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8052a59d-160b-523d-93b3-2be518aa22c8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-faaf0326-bcd6-5fdb-9b82-fb8c891b09c7" "answer-8052a59d-160b-523d-93b3-2be518aa22c8" "answer-5c1e780f-72c4-55fe-86bf-f63cbb6742d6" "answer-67143172-5c75-57e2-9507-5548168537b5" "answer-2dd7620a-2e0a-5048-84e8-580582cc4050"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-faaf0326-bcd6-5fdb-9b82-fb8c891b09c7" :answer/id #uuid "faaf0326-bcd6-5fdb-9b82-fb8c891b09c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8052a59d-160b-523d-93b3-2be518aa22c8" :answer/id #uuid "8052a59d-160b-523d-93b3-2be518aa22c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c1e780f-72c4-55fe-86bf-f63cbb6742d6" :answer/id #uuid "5c1e780f-72c4-55fe-86bf-f63cbb6742d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67143172-5c75-57e2-9507-5548168537b5" :answer/id #uuid "67143172-5c75-57e2-9507-5548168537b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{3}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2dd7620a-2e0a-5048-84e8-580582cc4050" :answer/id #uuid "2dd7620a-2e0a-5048-84e8-580582cc4050" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{2}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152111 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d5876a38-e0d7-5724-8ef9-3dbbcd7f0e46"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d5876a38-e0d7-5724-8ef9-3dbbcd7f0e46" :question/id #uuid "d5876a38-e0d7-5724-8ef9-3dbbcd7f0e46" :question/math-academy-id "q-163005" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4df58d2f35cc69e8a567a65bc64f24e5da8820e1be5d229979b08fe2c3d1cd8f.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a proportional relationship between the time $x$ (in months) since Mary's baby was born and the number $y$ of centimeters he has grown. What is the baby's unit rate of growth?" :question/worked-solution "The baby's unit rate of growth in centimeters per month is simply the constant of proportionality for the given proportional relationship.\n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(2,5).$\n\n![](images/5b/5bbc6c5b4188c5db894ceebe7d347c901afb428d5d102864a90218ef53420a60.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mary's baby's unit rate of growth is $\\frac{5}{2}$ centimeters per month." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0752f685-6167-5321-9403-2b36429faec1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0752f685-6167-5321-9403-2b36429faec1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0752f685-6167-5321-9403-2b36429faec1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2a00323d-f93f-5304-838e-9a2020861786" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3da9c120-60eb-589b-a9aa-5cbfc90e5257" "answer-b27a859b-f70f-5a32-ba4f-97460ef30f9d" "answer-2a00323d-f93f-5304-838e-9a2020861786" "answer-2f593f76-c5a7-50c8-9b17-9fcace1dabaf" "answer-9340f66f-9d6d-54b6-808d-a0bf629f8943"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3da9c120-60eb-589b-a9aa-5cbfc90e5257" :answer/id #uuid "3da9c120-60eb-589b-a9aa-5cbfc90e5257" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3$ centimeters per month" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b27a859b-f70f-5a32-ba4f-97460ef30f9d" :answer/id #uuid "b27a859b-f70f-5a32-ba4f-97460ef30f9d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{2}{5}$ centimeters per month" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a00323d-f93f-5304-838e-9a2020861786" :answer/id #uuid "2a00323d-f93f-5304-838e-9a2020861786" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{2}$ centimeters per month" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f593f76-c5a7-50c8-9b17-9fcace1dabaf" :answer/id #uuid "2f593f76-c5a7-50c8-9b17-9fcace1dabaf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{6}{5}$ centimeters per month" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9340f66f-9d6d-54b6-808d-a0bf629f8943" :answer/id #uuid "9340f66f-9d6d-54b6-808d-a0bf629f8943" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\frac{7}{3}$ centimeters per month" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-00211bb8-0554-55a4-bda1-bdbf208e3686" :activity/id #uuid "00211bb8-0554-55a4-bda1-bdbf208e3686" :activity/title "Understanding Proportional Relationships Using Graphs" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048993 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-96b62d68-1935-5cf6-bfed-287d64594d22" "step-4bbe34c0-1b18-5560-8226-a31de4f5850c" "step-3d3d364f-3ab5-5714-9335-f8bd97fe1c65"] :activity/first-step "step-96b62d68-1935-5cf6-bfed-287d64594d22" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-96b62d68-1935-5cf6-bfed-287d64594d22" :step/id #uuid "96b62d68-1935-5cf6-bfed-287d64594d22" :step/content 17592186152609 :step/next "step-4bbe34c0-1b18-5560-8226-a31de4f5850c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4bbe34c0-1b18-5560-8226-a31de4f5850c" :step/id #uuid "4bbe34c0-1b18-5560-8226-a31de4f5850c" :step/content "question-817c5709-21ea-5c46-9e06-c595f5917c73" :step/next "step-3d3d364f-3ab5-5714-9335-f8bd97fe1c65" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3d3d364f-3ab5-5714-9335-f8bd97fe1c65" :step/id #uuid "3d3d364f-3ab5-5714-9335-f8bd97fe1c65" :step/content "question-d5876a38-e0d7-5724-8ef9-3dbbcd7f0e46" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186125263 :question/answer-fields 17592186124178] {:db/id 17592186362338 :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=10\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨3,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}3=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{3}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=10$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{10}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =10 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 3,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-3,1),\\,(3,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-3,1)\\,$ | $\\,(3,-1)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,16\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(3,-1),$ and its maximum value is $16.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186362339 :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,16⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,8y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4=2\\lambda x \\\\ 16=8\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=4$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+4\\cdot \\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{4+16}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =4 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\,y=\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-8\\sqrt{5}\\,$ | $\\,8\\sqrt{5}\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}),$ and its minimum value is $-8\\sqrt{5}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281549 :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=8x-{y}^{2}=1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8,-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-4=8\\lambda  \\\\ 1=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2(-\\frac{1}{2})}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expression for $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1$ and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-{y}^{2}=1 \\\\ 8x-1 & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{4},1).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens to the right, there isn't a minimum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the maximum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{4},1)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{1}{4},1),$ and its maximum value is $4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281536 :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-2),(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2+1)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(2,0),(-2,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2),\\,(\\pm 2,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 2)$ | $(\\pm 2,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-3$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,\\pm 2)$ and its minimum value is $-3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125263 :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8x,4y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}8x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 4y=\\lambda (2y-2)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(4-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda -2)=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2y & =0\\,⇒\\,y=0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0),(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =4$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=4\\,⇒\\,y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $5$ | $13$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $(0,2)$ and its maximum value is $13.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281540 :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2=-2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}1-x\\lambda =0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-3 & =0\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+x(-1)=0\\,⇒\\,x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-1,-2),(1,2).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,-2),\\,(1,2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(1,-2)$ | $(1,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the points $(1,\\pm 2)$ and its maximum value is $-2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281534 :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=8\\lambda x \\\\ 2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-4\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(1-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-8 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-2\\sqrt{2}),(0,2\\sqrt{2}).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1-\\frac{1}{4})=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-\\sqrt{2},0),(\\sqrt{2},0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0),\\,(0,\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ | $(0,\\pm 2\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $4$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ and its minimum value is $4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281545 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={z}^{2}-xy-4=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-y,-x,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=-\\lambda y \\\\ 2y=-\\lambda x \\\\ 2z=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}(4-{\\lambda }^{2})x=0 \\\\ (4-{\\lambda }^{2})y=0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne 1$ and $\\lambda \\ne \\pm 2.$ Then, the solution of the above system is $x=y=z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0.$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =-2.$ Then we must have $x=y$ and $z=0.$ But this also contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0.$\n\n- Now, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the first and second equations, we must have $x=y=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}-4=0\\,⇒\\,z=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =2.$ Then we must have $x=-y$ and $z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-(-y)y-4=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(∓2,\\pm 2,0).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,0,\\pm 2)\\,$ | $\\,(∓2,\\pm 2,0)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,11\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,0,\\pm 2),$ and its minimum value is $7.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281542 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)=9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=36.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=36\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨18x,8y,2z⟩ \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨9x,4y,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=9\\lambda x \\\\ y=4\\lambda y \\\\ z=\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-9\\lambda )x=0 \\\\ (1-4\\lambda )y=0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne \\frac{1}{9},$ $\\lambda \\ne \\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\lambda \\ne 1.$ Then, the solution of the above system is $x=y=z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36.$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{9}.$ Then, by the second and third equations, we must have $y=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}=36\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0,0).$\n\n- Now, assume that $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}.$ Then, by the first and third equations, we must have $x=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n4{y}^{2}=36\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 3,0).$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the first and second equations, we must have $x=y=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}=36\\,⇒\\,z=\\pm 6.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0,\\pm 6).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(\\pm 2,0,0)\\,$ | $\\,(0,\\pm 3,0)\\,$ | $\\,(0,0,\\pm 6)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,9\\,$ | $\\,36\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm 2,0,0),$ and its minimum value is $4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186362343 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-z,-y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -z=2\\lambda y \\\\ -y=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda -1)=0 \\\\ y(4{\\lambda }^{2}-1)=0 \\\\ z=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne 1$ and $\\lambda \\ne \\pm \\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, the solution of the above system is $x=y=z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1.$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the second and third equations, we must have $y=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(\\pm 1,0,0).$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, by the first and third equations, we must have $x=0$ and $z=∓y.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+(-y)^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding values of $z$ are\n\n$$\nz=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(\\pm 1,0,0)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\,$ | $\\,∓\\frac{1}{2}\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the points $(\\pm 1,0,0),$  and its maximum value is $1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186362344 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=35.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=35\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,6,10⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2=2\\lambda x \\\\ 6=2\\lambda y \\\\ 10=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{\\lambda } \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=35$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =35 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{25}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =35 \\\\ \\frac{35}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =35 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\,y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1\\,y=\\pm 3\\,z=\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-1,-3,-5),\\,(1,3,5).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(-1,-3,-5)\\,$ | $\\,(1,3,5)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,-70\\,$ | $\\,70\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,3,5),$ and its maximum value is $70.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
